window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"file": "00-concepts.html", "id": "objects", "title": "Overall Concepts List · 1. Objects: what is random, and at what level?", "text": "Population / DGP The distribution that generates observations; its features \\(\\mu,\\sigma^2,p,\\beta\\) are fixed but usually unknown. Random sample Before observation, \\(Y_1,\\ldots,Y_n\\) are random variables. After observation, \\(y_1,\\ldots,y_n\\) are realized numbers. Statistic / estimator A function of the sample, such as \\(\\bar Y\\). Its sampling distribution describes repetition across hypothetical samples. Outcome, event, random variable An outcome is a primitive result; an event is a set of outcomes; a random variable maps outcomes to numbers. Example: two coin tosses produce outcome HT, event “one head,” and variable \\(Y=1\\). An event has probability; a realized numerical value is not itself a distribution. See 01 . Parameter, estimator, estimate \\(\\mu\\) is a population parameter; \\(\\bar Y\\) is the random estimator; 7.3 is one realized estimate. A standard error describes estimator uncertainty, not the spread of individual \\(Y\\). See 07 and 08 . Population distribution vs. sampling distribution The first describes variation across people/items; the second describes variation of a statistic across repeated samples. If \\(\\operatorname{SD}(Y)=12\\) and \\(n=36\\), then \\(\\operatorname{SD}(\\bar Y)=2\\) under i.i.d. sampling. Increasing \\(n\\) narrows the sampling distribution; it does not make the population less heterogeneous."}, {"file": "00-concepts.html", "id": "probability", "title": "Overall Concepts List · 2. Probability, distributions, and moments", "text": "PMF, PDF, CDF, quantile A PMF assigns mass to discrete values; a PDF gives density, so probability is area; the CDF \\(F(c)=P(Y\\le c)\\) works for both; a \\(q\\)-quantile is a threshold reaching cumulative probability \\(q\\). For continuous \\(Y\\), \\(f(y)\\) may exceed 1 and \\(P(Y=y)=0\\); neither fact violates probability rules. Quantiles need not be unique in discrete distributions. See 01 . Expectation, variance, standard deviation, moment Expectation is probability-weighted location; variance is expected squared deviation; SD restores the original units. Raw and central moments encode shape. Example: adding 10 changes the mean but not variance; multiplying by \\(-2\\) multiplies variance by 4 and SD by 2. \\(\\mathbb E[g(Y)]\\ne g(\\mathbb E[Y])\\) in general, and \\(\\operatorname{Var}(a+bY)=b^2\\operatorname{Var}(Y)\\). See 02 . Joint, marginal, and conditional distribution A joint distribution describes \\((X,Y)\\); summing/integrating out \\(X\\) yields the marginal of \\(Y\\); conditioning renormalizes within \\(X=x\\). Example: the overall employment rate is a weighted average of group-specific rates. \\(P(Y\\mid X)\\) is a random function of \\(X\\); \\(P(Y\\mid X=x)\\) is evaluated at one value. Conditioning can change both mean and variance. See 03 . Law of iterated expectations (LIE) \\(\\mathbb E[Y]=\\mathbb E[\\mathbb E[Y\\mid X]]\\): average within groups, then average across groups. The outer expectation uses the distribution of \\(X\\), so an unweighted average of group means is generally wrong. Conditional expectation function (CEF) \\(m(x)=\\mathbb E[Y\\mid X=x]\\) is the minimum-MSE predictor of \\(Y\\) among all functions of \\(X\\). The residual \\(u=Y-m(X)\\) satisfies \\(\\mathbb E[u\\mid X]=0\\). The CEF need not be linear; zero conditional mean is stronger than zero covariance. See 03 ."}, {"file": "00-concepts.html", "id": "dependence", "title": "Overall Concepts List · 3. Dependence: the implication ladder", "text": "\\[X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Y\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\\mathbb E[Y\\mid X]=\\mathbb E[Y]\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=0.\\] The reverse arrows fail without extra assumptions. Example: with symmetric \\(X\\), let \\(Y=X^2\\). Then \\(\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=0\\), but knowing \\(X\\) determines \\(Y\\). In a jointly normal family, zero covariance does imply independence. Independence, mean independence, uncorrelatedness Independence preserves the whole conditional distribution; mean independence preserves only the conditional mean; zero covariance removes only linear co-movement. “No correlation” is not “no relationship.” See 04 . Covariance and correlation Covariance records direction and scale of linear co-movement; correlation standardizes it to \\([-1,1]\\). Example: changing dollars to cents changes covariance but not correlation. Correlation is unit-free but not causal, is sensitive to outliers, and may miss nonlinear dependence. Variance of a sum and diversification Variances add only when covariances are zero. Positively correlated assets reduce diversification; negative covariance can offset risk. Independence is sufficient, not necessary, for covariance zero; pairwise independence and mutual independence are distinct."}, {"file": "00-concepts.html", "id": "asymptotics", "title": "Overall Concepts List · 4. Sampling, approximation, and estimation quality", "text": "i.i.d. and simple random sampling Identically distributed means a common marginal distribution; independent means one draw provides no information about another. Sampling without replacement from a finite population is not literally independent; clustered or time-series observations require different variance reasoning. See 06 . Law of large numbers (LLN) The sample mean converges in probability to its population mean under suitable conditions. It justifies replacing population moments by sample analogues. LLN says concentration, not normality, and does not give the finite-sample error probability by itself. Central limit theorem (CLT) A standardized sum becomes approximately normal: \\(\\sqrt n(\\bar Y-\\mu)/\\sigma\\Rightarrow\\mathcal N(0,1)\\). “Large \\(n\\)” is distribution-dependent; skewness, heavy tails, dependence, and rare Bernoulli outcomes can slow approximation. The CLT concerns the statistic, not the raw data. Exact vs. asymptotic distribution Normal data give an exact Student \\(t\\) pivot for a mean with unknown variance; the CLT gives an approximation for broader populations. Writing \\(\\sim\\) claims an exact distribution; \\(\\approx\\) or \\(\\xrightarrow{d}\\) communicates approximation or a limit. See 11 . Unbiasedness, consistency, efficiency, MSE Unbiasedness is an expectation statement at a fixed \\(n\\); consistency is convergence as \\(n\\to\\infty\\); efficiency compares variability under a stated class; \\(\\operatorname{MSE}=\\operatorname{Var}+\\operatorname{Bias}^2\\). An unbiased estimator can be noisy or inconsistent; a slightly biased estimator can have lower MSE. “BLUE” means best linear unbiased under its assumptions, not best among every imaginable estimator. See 07 . Standard deviation, standard error, estimated standard error SD is a population spread; SE is the true SD of an estimator; the reported SE is usually an estimate using \\(s\\). The \\(n-1\\) divisor corrects variance-estimator bias; it is not the sample size used in \\(\\operatorname{SE}(\\bar Y)=s/\\sqrt n\\). See 08 . Studentization, continuous mapping, Slutsky Studentization divides estimation error by an estimated SD. Consistency of \\(s\\) plus Slutsky lets an estimated denominator replace \\(\\sigma\\) asymptotically. A consistent denominator does not magically make a small-sample statistic exactly \\(t\\); exactness needs normality and the independence result."}, {"file": "00-concepts.html", "id": "inference", "title": "Overall Concepts List · 5. Inference and causality", "text": "Null, alternative, test statistic, rejection region The null is the benchmark restriction; the statistic measures disagreement in standard-error units; the rejection region is chosen before seeing the data. Failure to reject is not proof that the null is true. A two-sided alternative splits \\(\\alpha\\) across two tails. See 09 . p-value, size/significance level, power The p-value is the probability, assuming the null, of a statistic at least as incompatible with it as observed. Size is the null rejection probability; power is rejection probability under a specified alternative. A p-value is neither \\(P(H_0\\mid\\text{data})\\) nor effect size. Lowering \\(\\alpha\\) reduces false positives but, holding \\(n\\) fixed, also lowers power. Confidence level and coverage A 95% procedure covers the fixed parameter in 95% of repeated samples under its assumptions. One realized interval either covers or does not. The frequentist statement is about the procedure, not a 95% probability assigned to the already-fixed parameter. Narrowness is not validity. Statistical vs. economic significance A tiny effect may be precisely estimated and statistically significant; a consequential effect may be imprecisely estimated. Report effect size, units, interval, and design. Large samples can make substantively trivial differences yield small p-values. Independent groups vs. paired data Independent samples use two group variances; paired analysis first forms within-pair differences \\(D_i\\), then performs one-sample inference. Treating paired observations as independent discards covariance and usually changes the SE. Equal group sizes do not imply equal variances. See 10 . Potential outcomes, ATE, random assignment, selection bias Each unit has \\(Y(1),Y(0)\\), but only one is observed. The ATE is \\(\\mathbb E[Y(1)-Y(0)]\\). Random assignment makes treatment independent of potential outcomes, so a difference in means identifies the ATE in expectation. A raw treated-control difference mixes treatment effects and selection when assignment is not random. Random sampling supports population generalization; random assignment supports causal identification—different jobs. See 10 ."}, {"file": "00-concepts.html", "id": "regression", "title": "Overall Concepts List · 6. Regression and resampling bridges", "text": "Population regression, OLS, residual, error The population linear projection chooses coefficients minimizing population MSE; OLS is its sample analogue. A residual \\(\\hat u_i\\) is observable after fitting; the population error \\(u_i\\) is not. A fitted line is descriptive unless an identification argument gives its slope a causal interpretation. See 12 . Exogeneity and omitted-variable bias Mean independence \\(\\mathbb E[u\\mid X]=0\\) implies zero covariance; for slope consistency, the key orthogonality is \\(\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E X)u]=0\\). If an omitted cause is correlated with \\(X\\), the slope absorbs some of its association. Zero sample correlation between fitted residuals and included \\(X\\) is an OLS algebraic property; it does not prove population exogeneity. Homoskedasticity and heteroskedasticity Homoskedasticity means \\(\\operatorname{Var}(u\\mid X)=\\sigma^2\\); heteroskedasticity lets it vary with \\(X\\). Heteroskedasticity need not bias OLS coefficients under exogeneity, but conventional homoskedastic SEs can be wrong. Bootstrap and empirical distribution The nonparametric bootstrap resamples observed cases with replacement, recomputes the statistic, and uses its conditional distribution to approximate sampling uncertainty. Resample the observational unit (pairs for regression, clusters for clustered designs). Bootstrap validity is not automatic for nonsmooth estimators, boundaries, extreme tails, or dependence. See 13 ."}, {"file": "00-concepts.html", "id": "check", "title": "Overall Concepts List · 7. Thirty-second self-check", "text": "Can I name the random object and its hypothetical repetitions? Am I claiming equality, exact distribution, approximation, or convergence? Which assumption delivers the conclusion: independence, mean independence, zero covariance, normality, or large \\(n\\)? Does the number describe individual variation, estimator uncertainty, effect magnitude, or evidence against a null? Is a comparison descriptive or causal, and what makes the counterfactual credible?"}, {"file": "01-probability.html", "id": "objects", "title": "Probability Distributions · 1. Outcomes, events, and random variables", "text": "An outcome is a possible result of a random process; the sample space \\(\\Omega\\) contains all outcomes. An event A is a subset of \\(\\Omega . A\\) random variable \\(Y\\) maps an outcome to a numerical value. Probabilities are nonnegative\\(, \\Pr(\\Omega )=1\\), and probabilities of mutually exclusive events add. \\[P(A\\cup B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B),\\qquad P(A^c)=1-P(A).\\] A discrete variable takes a finite or countable set of values. A continuous variable with a density takes values on a continuum. Uppercase \\(Y\\) denotes the random variable; lowercase \\(y\\) denotes a possible or realized value. A parameter such as \\(\\mu\\) describes its population distribution. 中文直觉：事件是“会不会发生的事”，随机变量是把结果变成可以运算的数字。例如“通勤超过 20 分钟”是事件，通勤分钟数是随机变量。概率不是每次都必须兑现的比例，而是对重复过程的描述。"}, {"file": "01-probability.html", "id": "discrete", "title": "Probability Distributions · 2. PMF, CDF, and the Bernoulli distribution", "text": "The probability mass function (PMF) is \\(p_{Y}(y)=\\Pr(Y=y)\\). For support \\({y_{1},\\ldots ,y_{k}}\\), each probability is nonnegative and their sum is one. Event probabilities are sums over the relevant support points. \\[F_Y(c)=P(Y\\le c)=\\sum_{y\\le c}p_Y(y).\\] The cumulative distribution function (CDF) is nondecreasing and right-continuous, with limits zero and one at the two extremes. For any \\(a<b, \\Pr(a<Y\\le b)=F_{Y}(b)-F_{Y}(a)\\). At a discrete mass point\\(, \\Pr(Y=a)=F_{Y}(a)-F_{Y}(a-)\\); endpoint inclusion matters. Textbook crash example, SW Table 2.1 Number of crashes M 0 1 2 3 4 \\(\\Pr(M=m)\\) .80 .10 .06 .03 .01 \\(F_{M}(m)\\) .80 .90 .96 .99 1 Thus \\(\\Pr(1\\le M\\le 2)=.10+.06=.16\\), whereas \\(\\Pr(1<M\\le 2)=.06\\). The CDF is a staircase, with jump sizes equal to the PMF. A Bernoulli variable equals 1 with probability \\(p\\) and 0 with probability \\(1-p\\). It encodes any binary outcome: employment, voting, treatment assignment, or a short commute. Its parameter is a probability, not an observed sample fraction."}, {"file": "01-probability.html", "id": "continuous", "title": "Probability Distributions · 3. Continuous distributions and density", "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned} f_Y(y)&\\ge0, & \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}f_Y(y)\\,dy&=1,\\\\ F_Y(c)&=\\int_{-\\infty}^{c}f_Y(y)\\,dy,\\\\ P(a<Y\\le b)&=\\int_a^b f_Y(y)\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\\] For a variable with a density\\(, \\Pr(Y=c)=0\\). The density is a height, not a point probability, and can exceed one. For example, the uniform density on [0,.5] has height 2; the total area is still one. Where F is differentiable\\(, f=F'\\). 中文直觉：CDF 像累计里程表，记录“到这里为止积累了多少概率”；PDF 像每单位横轴上的概率浓度。连续型随机变量要问一个区间内的面积，不能把曲线高度当作概率。 Worked example. If the commuting-time CDF satisfies \\(F(15)=.20\\) and \\(F(20)=.78\\), then \\(\\Pr(15<Y\\le 20)=.58\\) and \\(\\Pr(Y>20)=.22\\). Both a CDF difference and a density area describe the same event."}, {"file": "01-probability.html", "id": "quantiles", "title": "Probability Distributions · 4. Quantiles and distribution reading", "text": "The \\(\\tau\\)-quantile is \\(q_\\tau=\\inf\\{y:F(y)\\ge\\tau\\}\\). The median is \\(q_{.5}\\); the interquartile range is \\(q_{.75}-q_{.25}\\). For continuous strictly increasing CDFs, \\(F(q_\\tau)=\\tau\\). The generalized definition also works for discrete variables. Percentiles become critical values in inference: the 97.5th percentile of a standard normal distribution leaves 2.5% in its upper tail. A median describes a distribution'\\(s\\) location but need not equal its mean. Check your understanding Question. A density equals 3 on an interval of width .1. What is the probability of that interval? Can a discrete PMF equal 3? Solution. The probability is .3. A PMF cannot exceed 1, because each value is an event probability. Sources: SW §2.1, printed pp. 61–65 (PDF pp. 2–6); percentile interpretation in Table 2.4, p. 80. Quantile definition and density calculus are supporting extensions. Next: moments turn distributions into useful numerical summaries ."}, {"file": "02-moments.html", "id": "expectation", "title": "Expectation, Moments & Shape · 1. Expectation is a probability-weighted average", "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}\\mathbb E[Y]&=\\sum_j y_jp_j &&\\text{(discrete)},\\\\ \\mathbb E[Y]&=\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}y f_Y(y)\\,dy &&\\text{(density)},\\\\ \\mathbb E[g(Y)]&=\\sum_j g(y_j)p_j\\\\&\\quad\\text{or }\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}g(y)f_Y(y)\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\\] These expectations require the relevant integrability conditions; \\(\\mathbb{E}|Y|<\\infty\\) ensures a finite mean. The expectation need not be a possible observation. In the crash example\\(, \\mathbb{E}[M]=.35\\) even though no computer crashes .35 times. A repayment of 110 with probability .99 and zero otherwise has expected repayment 108.90; the expected net payoff after lending 100 is 8.90. Linearity: \\(\\mathbb{E}[a+\\)bX+cY\\(]=a+\\)bE\\([X]+\\)cE[Y]. Independence is unnecessary. However\\(, \\mathbb{E}[g(Y)]\\) generally differs from \\(g(\\mathbb{E}[Y])\\). For example\\(, \\mathbb{E}[Y^{2}]\\) differs from \\((\\mathbb{E}[Y])^{2}\\) whenever \\(Y\\) has positive variance. 中文直觉：期望是把每种结果按出现频率加权后的重心。它既不是“最可能的结果”，也不是“保证拿到的结果”。非线性变换会改变权重效果，所以“先平均再平方”和“先平方再平均”不是一回事。"}, {"file": "02-moments.html", "id": "variance", "title": "Expectation, Moments & Shape · 2. Variance and standard deviation", "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}\\mu&=\\mathbb E[Y],\\\\ \\sigma^2&=\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=\\mathbb E[(Y-\\mu)^2]\\\\&=\\mathbb E[Y^2]-\\mu^2,\\\\ \\sigma&=\\operatorname{SD}(Y)=\\sqrt{\\operatorname{Var}(Y)}.\\end{aligned}\\] Variance is nonnegative and is zero only when \\(Y\\) is constant with probability one. It has squared units; standard deviation has the units of \\(Y\\). Expanding \\((Y-\\mu )^{2}\\) gives \\(\\mathbb{E}[Y^{2}]-2\\mu \\mathbb{E}[Y]+\\mu ^{2}=\\mathbb{E}[Y^{2}]-\\mu ^{2}\\). Worked example. For M in Module \\(1, \\mathbb{E}[M^{2}]=.10+4(.06)+9(.03)+16(.01)=.77\\). Therefore \\(\\operatorname{Var}(M)=.77-.35^{2}=.6475\\) and \\(\\operatorname{SD}(M)\\approx .8047\\). Bernoulli. Since \\(Y^{2}=Y, \\mathbb{E}[Y]=p, \\mathbb{E}[Y^{2}]=p\\), and \\(\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=p(1-p)\\). Variance is largest at \\(p=.5\\) and vanishes at \\(p=0\\) or 1. This identity later supplies the sampling uncertainty of a sample proportion."}, {"file": "02-moments.html", "id": "transforms", "title": "Expectation, Moments & Shape · 3. Affine transformations and units", "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}\\mathbb E[a+bY]&=a+b\\mu,\\\\ \\operatorname{Var}(a+bY)&=b^2\\sigma^2,\\\\ \\operatorname{SD}(a+bY)&=|b|\\sigma.\\end{aligned}\\] Derivation. Subtracting the new mean leaves \\(a+\\)bY\\(-(a+b\\mu )=b(Y-\\mu )\\). Squaring and taking expectation produces \\(b^{2}\\sigma ^{2}\\). Taking the nonnegative square root gives \\(|b|\\sigma\\), including when b is negative. If after-tax income is \\(2000+.8X\\), the mean becomes \\(2000+.8\\mu _{X}\\) and the standard deviation becomes \\(.8\\sigma _{X}. A\\) fixed transfer changes location but not spread. Converting dollars to cents multiplies means and standard deviations by 100 and variances by 10,000."}, {"file": "02-moments.html", "id": "shape", "title": "Expectation, Moments & Shape · 4. Moments, skewness, kurtosis, and outliers", "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Raw }r\\text{th moment: }&\\mathbb E[Y^r],\\\\ \\text{Central }r\\text{th moment: }&\\mathbb E[(Y-\\mu)^r],\\\\ \\operatorname{Skewness}(Y)&=\\frac{\\mathbb E[(Y-\\mu)^3]}{\\sigma^3},\\\\ \\operatorname{Kurtosis}(Y)&=\\frac{\\mathbb E[(Y-\\mu)^4]}{\\sigma^4}.\\end{aligned}\\] Standardized moments require \\(\\sigma >0\\) and existence of the relevant moment. Positive skewness commonly signals a long right tail; negative skewness commonly signals a long left tail. Symmetry implies zero skewness when the third moment exists. The converse is not a general theorem: an asymmetric distribution can have zero third central moment. Normal kurtosis is 3; excess kurtosis subtracts 3. High kurtosis indicates a large standardized fourth moment and sensitivity to extreme observations. It does not rank every tail probability at every cutoff. Two distributions can share a mean and variance while differing greatly in shape and outlier frequency. Positive changes of units preserve skewness and kurtosis; multiplying by a negative number reverses skewness. \\[\\begin{aligned}\\mathbb E[(Y-\\mu)^3]&=\\mathbb E[Y^3]-3\\mu\\mathbb E[Y^2]+2\\mu^3,\\\\ \\mathbb E[(Y-\\mu)^4]&=\\mathbb E[Y^4]-4\\mu\\mathbb E[Y^3]\\\\&\\quad+6\\mu^2\\mathbb E[Y^2]-3\\mu^4.\\end{aligned}\\] These expansions explain why statements about finite fourth moments appear later: applying a finite-variance LLN to \\(Y^{2}\\) requires \\(\\operatorname{Var}(Y^{2})<\\infty\\). The moment restriction belongs to a theorem'\\(s\\) justification, not merely to a descriptive statistic. 中文直觉：平均数告诉你中心，方差告诉你整体波动，偏度告诉你左右是否不对称，峰度对极端偏离特别敏感。四个数字不是整张分布图；看到同样的均值、方差，不能断定两个总体一样。"}, {"file": "02-moments.html", "id": "applications", "title": "Expectation, Moments & Shape · 5. Earnings and model adequacy", "text": "SW Table 2.4 compares earnings conditional on education and gender. For women with a high-school diploma, the reported mean is 15.49 and median 14.03; for women with a four-year degree, they are 25.42 and 22.44. The comparison includes dispersion and upper percentiles, not just a mean shift. These are historical textbook summaries of 2012 full-time workers, not current estimates or causal education effects. The stock-return box in §2.4 supplies a second lesson: matching typical variation with a normal distribution can badly understate extreme outcomes. This concerns the distribution of individual observations; a CLT for averages is a separate claim. Time-varying volatility and heavy tails are two distinct ways to reconsider the model. Check your understanding Question. If \\(\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=9\\), what is \\(\\operatorname{SD}(10-2Y)\\)? Is \\(\\mathbb{E}[Y^{2}]=9\\)? Solution. \\(\\operatorname{SD}(10-2Y)=6. \\mathbb{E}[Y^{2}]=9+\\mu ^{2}\\), which equals 9 only if \\(\\mu =0\\). Sources: SW §2.2, pp. 65–71; Table 2.4, pp. 79–80; stock-return box, pp. 85–86; Exercises 2.21 and Appendix 2.1. General sign and moment caveats are explicit mathematical clarifications."}, {"file": "03-conditioning.html", "id": "joint", "title": "Conditional Information & Prediction · 1. Joint \\(\\to\\) marginal \\(\\to\\) conditional distributions", "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}p_{X,Y}(x,y)&=P(X=x,Y=y),\\\\ p_Y(y)&=\\sum_x p_{X,Y}(x,y),\\\\ p_{Y\\mid X}(y\\mid x)&=\\frac{p_{X,Y}(x,y)}{p_X(x)},\\quad p_X(x)>0.\\end{aligned}\\] Marginalization adds over the variable no longer being tracked. Conditioning restricts attention to a subgroup and renormalizes its probabilities to sum to one. For variables with a joint density, replace sums by integrals and use \\(f_{Y|X}(y|x)=f_{X,Y}(x,y)/f_{X}(x)\\) where the denominator is positive. Do not divide by \\(\\Pr(X=x)=0\\) in the continuous case. SW Table 2.2: \\(X=1\\) means no rain; \\(Y=1\\) means short commute \\(X=0\\) \\(X=1\\) Marginal \\(Y=0\\) .15 .07 .22 \\(Y=1\\) .15 .63 .78 Marginal .30 .70 1 \\(\\Pr(Y=1\\mid X=0)=.15/.30=.5\\), while \\(\\Pr(Y=1\\mid X=1)=.63/.70=.9\\). Reversing the conditioning gives a different question: \\(\\Pr(X=0\\mid Y=1)=.15/.78\\approx .1923\\). Bayes' rule (supporting connection). \\(\\Pr(X=x|Y=y)=\\Pr(Y=y|X=x)\\Pr(X=x)/\\Pr(Y=y)\\). It follows by expressing the same joint probability in two orders. A large \\(\\Pr(Y\\mid X)\\) does not automatically imply a large \\(\\Pr(X\\mid Y)\\). 中文直觉：边际分布是“不管另一项是什么，全部加起来”；条件分布是“只看符合条件的人或日子，再把这组看成 100%”。先明确分母是谁，就不容易把条件概率倒过来。"}, {"file": "03-conditioning.html", "id": "conditional-mean", "title": "Conditional Information & Prediction · 2. Conditional mean and conditional variance", "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}m(x)&=\\mathbb E[Y\\mid X=x]\\\\&=\\sum_y y\\,p_{Y\\mid X}(y\\mid x),\\\\ \\operatorname{Var}(Y\\mid X=x)&=\\mathbb E[(Y-m(x))^2\\mid X=x].\\end{aligned}\\] \\(\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X=x]\\) is a number for a fixed \\(x. \\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X]=m(X)\\) is a random variable because the information \\(X\\) varies. Conditional variance measures dispersion around the relevant conditional mean, not the unconditional mean. For the commuting table\\(, \\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X=0]=.5\\) and \\(\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X=1]=.9\\); the corresponding conditional variances are .25 and .09. Both the conditional mean and the conditional spread change with weather."}, {"file": "03-conditioning.html", "id": "lie", "title": "Conditional Information & Prediction · 3. Law of iterated expectations", "text": "\\[\\mathbb E[Y]=\\mathbb E[\\mathbb E[Y\\mid X]]=\\sum_x m(x)p_X(x).\\] Assumption. \\(\\mathbb{E}|Y|<\\infty\\). Discrete proof. Substitute the conditional mean and multiply the conditional probability by \\(p_{X}(x)\\). The result is \\(\\sum _{x}\\sum _{y}y p_{X,Y}(x,y)=\\sum _{y}y p_{Y}(y)=\\mathbb{E}[Y]\\). In the commuting example\\(, \\mathbb{E}[Y]=.5(.3)+.9(.7)=.78\\). With multiple conditioning variables\\(, \\mathbb{E}[Y]=\\mathbb{E}[\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X,Z]]\\). The nested form \\(\\mathbb{E}[\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X,Z]\\mid X]=\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X]\\) averages away the additional information \\(Z\\) while retaining \\(X\\). If h(X) is known conditional on \\(X, \\mathbb{E}[h(X)Y|X]=h(X)\\mathbb{E}[Y|X]\\), provided the relevant products are integrable. Therefore \\(\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X]=0\\) implies \\(\\mathbb{E}[Y]=0\\) and \\(\\mathbb{E}[h(X)Y]=0\\). This implication is central to regression moment conditions. 中文直觉：先算每组平均数，再按各组人数比例加权，等于直接算总体平均数。不能不管组大小就简单平均。多重条件期望就是先分得更细，再逐步合并。"}, {"file": "03-conditioning.html", "id": "prediction", "title": "Conditional Information & Prediction · 4. Conditional expectation as the best predictor", "text": "This chain is developed in SW Exercise 2.27 and made explicit here. Assume \\(\\mathbb{E}[Y^{2}]<\\infty\\). Let \\(m(X)=\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X]\\) and \\(u=Y-m(X)\\). Then \\(\\mathbb{E}[u\\mid X]=0\\) and \\(\\mathbb{E}[u h(X)]=0\\) for square-integrable h(X). \\[\\begin{aligned}\\mathbb E[(Y-g(X))^2]&=\\mathbb E[u^2]+\\mathbb E[(m(X)-g(X))^2]\\\\&\\ge\\mathbb E[u^2].\\end{aligned}\\] Proof. Write \\(Y-g(X)=u+m(X)-g(X)\\), square, and take expectations. The cross term vanishes because \\(\\mathbb{E}[u\\mid X]=0\\). Thus \\(m(X)\\) minimizes mean squared prediction error over all square-integrable functions of \\(X\\); equality holds when \\(g(X)=m(X)\\) almost surely. Setting the alternative predictor to the constant \\(\\mathbb{E}[Y]\\) gives the law of total variance : \\[\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=\\mathbb E[\\operatorname{Var}(Y\\mid X)]+\\operatorname{Var}(\\mathbb E[Y\\mid X]).\\] For the commute table: \\(.78(.22)=.3(.25)+.7(.09)+.3(.5-.78)^{2}+.7(.9-.78)^{2}=.138+.0336=.1716\\). 中文直觉：知道天气后，组内仍有不可预测的波动；不同天气的平均通勤情况也有差别。总体波动=组内剩余波动+组间均值波动。回归就是把这种“用信息改善预测”的思路具体化，但最优预测不自动等于因果解释。 Check your understanding Question. If \\(\\mathbb{E}[u\\mid X]=0\\), must \\(\\operatorname{Var}(u|X)\\) be constant? Solution. No. Take \\(u=X\\varepsilon\\) where \\(\\varepsilon\\) is independent of \\(X\\) with mean zero and variance one. Then \\(\\mathbb{E}[u\\mid X]=0\\) but \\(\\operatorname{Var}(u|X)=X^{2}\\). Sources: SW §2.3, pp. 72–78; Exercises 2.19–2.20 and 2.27, pp. 106–109. Total variance and Bayes' rule are supporting extensions. Continue to dependence and covariance algebra ."}, {"file": "04-covariance.html", "id": "hierarchy", "title": "Dependence & Covariance Algebra · 1. A hierarchy of dependence restrictions", "text": "Independence: the joint distribution factors into the marginals. Equivalently, knowing \\(X\\) does not change the distribution of \\(Y\\). Mean independence of \\(Y\\) from \\(X\\): \\(\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X]=\\mathbb{E}[Y]\\). Zero covariance: \\(\\mathbb{E}[(X-\\mu _{X})(Y-\\mu _{Y})]=0\\). With the required moments: \\[\\begin{gathered}X\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp Y\\\\ \\Downarrow\\\\ \\mathbb E[Y\\mid X]=\\mathbb E[Y]\\\\ \\Downarrow\\\\ \\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=0.\\end{gathered}\\] Mean independence is directional. Independence is symmetric. Neither reverse implication is generally valid. Counterexample to zero covariance \\(\\implies\\) mean independence. Let \\(X\\) and \\(Z\\) be independent \\(\\mathcal{N}(0,1)\\) and \\(Y=X^{2}+Z\\). Then \\(\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X]=X^{2}, \\mathbb{E}[Y]=1\\), and \\(\\mathbb{E}[\\)XY\\(]=\\mathbb{E}[X^{3}]+\\mathbb{E}[\\)XZ]=0. Thus \\(\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)=0\\) although \\(X\\) predicts the conditional mean of \\(Y\\). Counterexample to mean independence \\(\\implies\\) independence. Let \\(X\\) take values 1 and 2 with equal probability and let \\(\\varepsilon\\) independently take \\(\\pm 1\\) with equal probability. Set \\(Y=X\\varepsilon . \\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X]=0\\), but |Y| reveals \\(X\\). The conditional spread depends on \\(X\\)."}, {"file": "04-covariance.html", "id": "cov", "title": "Dependence & Covariance Algebra · 2. Covariance and correlation", "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}\\sigma_{XY}&=\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)\\\\&=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mu_X)(Y-\\mu_Y)]\\\\&=\\mathbb E[XY]-\\mu_X\\mu_Y,\\\\ \\rho_{XY}&=\\frac{\\sigma_{XY}}{\\sigma_X\\sigma_Y},\\qquad -1\\le\\rho_{XY}\\le1.\\end{aligned}\\] Correlation requires finite, positive variances. Covariance has product units; correlation is unitless. Correlation measures linear association, not slope or all forms of dependence. Correlation \\(\\pm 1\\) means an exact affine relationship almost surely, with positive or negative slope. Why mean independence implies zero covariance. \\(\\mathbb{E}[\\)XY\\(]=\\mathbb{E}[X \\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X]]=\\mathbb{E}[X]\\mathbb{E}[Y\\)], so subtracting \\(\\mu _{X}\\mu _{Y}\\) gives zero. This uses iterated expectations, not an assumption of full independence."}, {"file": "04-covariance.html", "id": "algebra", "title": "Dependence & Covariance Algebra · 3. Algebra for weighted sums", "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Cov}(a+bX+cV,Y)&=b\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)+c\\operatorname{Cov}(V,Y),\\\\ \\operatorname{Var}(aX+bY)&=a^2\\sigma_X^2+b^2\\sigma_Y^2+2ab\\sigma_{XY},\\\\ \\operatorname{Var}(X-Y)&=\\sigma_X^2+\\sigma_Y^2-2\\sigma_{XY},\\\\ \\operatorname{Var}\\!\\left(\\sum_iw_iY_i\\right)&=\\sum_iw_i^2\\operatorname{Var}(Y_i)\\\\&\\quad+2\\sum_{i<j}w_iw_j\\operatorname{Cov}(Y_i,Y_j).\\end{aligned}\\] Derivation. Center each variable, expand the square of the weighted sum, and take expectations term by term. Independence is needed only if it is used to set cross-covariances to zero. For a difference, the coefficients are 1 and \\(-1\\), which explains the minus sign on the covariance term. Correlation inequality, Appendix 2.1. For \\(\\sigma _{X}^{2}>0\\), nonnegativity of \\(\\operatorname{Var}(Y-\\)aX) at \\(a=\\sigma _{XY}/\\sigma _{X}^{2}\\) gives \\(0\\le \\sigma _{Y}^{2}-\\sigma _{XY}^{2}/\\sigma _{X}^{2}\\). Hence \\(\\sigma _{XY}^{2}\\le \\sigma _{X}^{2}\\sigma _{Y}^{2}\\) and \\(|\\rho |\\le 1\\). This is the covariance form of the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality."}, {"file": "04-covariance.html", "id": "diversification", "title": "Dependence & Covariance Algebra · 4. Diversification and dependence", "text": "For an equally weighted average of \\(n\\) variables with common mean \\(\\mu\\), common variance \\(\\sigma ^{2}\\), and common pairwise correlation \\(\\rho\\): \\[\\mathbb E[\\bar Y]=\\mu,\\qquad \\operatorname{Var}(\\bar Y)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}+\\frac{n-1}{n}\\rho\\sigma^2.\\] There are \\(n\\) variance terms and \\(n(n-1)\\) ordered covariance terms in the expanded square. If \\(\\rho =0\\), the variance falls as \\(1/n\\). For fixed positive \\(\\rho\\), it approaches \\(\\rho \\sigma ^{2}\\). The common-correlation covariance matrix must be valid; in particular \\(-1/(n-1)\\le \\rho \\le 1. A\\) fixed negative \\(\\rho\\) cannot persist for arbitrarily large \\(n\\). 中文直觉：许多彼此独立的小风险可以互相抵消；大家同时承受的共同冲击不会因为人数多就消失。这既是分散投资的边界，也解释了为什么同一个班、企业或地区里的观察不能随便当成独立样本。 Two-asset example. \\(R=\\)wR\\(_{s}+(1-w)R_{b}\\), with means .08 and .05, standard deviations .07 and .04, and correlation .25. At \\(w=.5, \\mathbb{E}[R]=.065\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Var}(R)=.001975\\), so \\(\\operatorname{SD}(R)\\approx .04444\\). The minimum-variance weight without additional constraints is \\(w*=(\\sigma _{b}^{2}-\\sigma _{sb})/(\\sigma _{s}^{2}+\\sigma _{b}^{2}-2\\sigma _{sb})\\approx .17647\\). This is a mathematical example using textbook inputs. Check your understanding Question. If two measurements have positive covariance, does subtracting one from the other increase or decrease the variance relative to independent measurements with the same marginal variances? Solution. It decreases it by \\(2Cov(X,Y)\\). This is why paired comparisons can be more precise. Sources: SW §2.3, pp. 77–81; diversification box p. 92; Exercises 2.22–2.23, 2.26; Appendix 2.1, pp. 109–110. Counterexample for mean independence and validity bound are supporting clarifications."}, {"file": "05-distributions.html", "id": "normal", "title": "Normal, χ², t & F · 1. Normal probabilities and standardization", "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}Y\\sim\\mathcal N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)&\\implies Z=\\frac{Y-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1),\\quad\\sigma>0,\\\\ \\Phi(c)&=P(Z\\le c),\\\\ P(a\\le Y\\le b)&=\\Phi\\!\\left(\\frac{b-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\right)-\\Phi\\!\\left(\\frac{a-\\mu}{\\sigma}\\right).\\end{aligned}\\] The second argument in \\(\\mathcal{N}(\\mu ,\\sigma ^{2})\\) is the variance. A normal distribution is symmetric, has skewness zero and kurtosis three. Its central 95% lies within approximately 1.96 standard deviations of its mean. Standardization always gives mean zero and variance one when these moments exist; it gives a standard normal distribution only when normality is justified. Example. If \\(Y\\sim \\mathcal{N}(1,4), \\operatorname{SD}(Y)=2\\) and \\(\\Pr(Y\\le 2)=\\Phi (.5)\\approx .6915. \\Pr(|Z|>c)=2[1-\\Phi (c)]\\) for \\(c\\ge 0\\). Symmetry gives \\(\\Phi (-c)=1-\\Phi (c)\\). Central probability Positive cutoff Area in each tail 90% 1.64485 5% 95% 1.95996 2.5% 99% 2.57583 .5% 中文直觉：标准化是在换坐标尺，把“偏离均值多少美元”变成“偏离均值多少个标准差”。换尺子不会自动把歪斜分布变成钟形。"}, {"file": "05-distributions.html", "id": "multivariate", "title": "Normal, χ², t & F · 2. Joint normality", "text": "A jointly normal vector has normal linear combinations. Consequently, each marginal is normal; aX+bY is normal with mean \\(a\\mu _{X}+b\\mu _{Y}\\) and variance \\(a^{2}\\sigma _{X}^{2}+b^{2}\\sigma _{Y}^{2}+2ab\\sigma _{XY}\\). For jointly normal variables, zero covariance implies independence. Merely having normal marginal distributions does not establish joint normality. For a nondegenerate bivariate normal distribution, the conditional mean is linear: \\[\\mathbb E[Y\\mid X=x]=\\mu_Y+\\frac{\\sigma_{XY}}{\\sigma_X^2}(x-\\mu_X).\\] Joint normality implies this form, but a linear conditional mean does not imply joint normality. The displayed slope also anticipates the population linear projection in Module 12."}, {"file": "05-distributions.html", "id": "family", "title": "Normal, χ², t & F · 3. Constructing \\(\\chi ^{2}\\), Student \\(t\\), and F", "text": "Distribution Construction Required independence \\(\\chi ^{2}_{m}\\) \\(W=Z_{1}^{2}+\\cdots +Z_{m}^{2}, Z_{j}\\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)\\) The \\(Z_{j}\\) are mutually independent \\(t_{m}\\) \\(T=Z/\\sqrt{W/m}, Z\\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1), W\\sim \\chi ^{2}_{m}\\) \\(Z\\) and \\(W\\) are independent \\(F_{m,k}\\) \\((W/m)/(V/k), W\\sim \\chi ^{2}_{m}, V\\sim \\chi ^{2}_{k}\\) \\(W\\) and V are independent The degrees of freedom count independent squared standard normals in the \\(\\chi ^{2}\\) construction\\(. \\chi ^{2}\\) and F are nonnegative; \\(t\\) is symmetric with heavier tails than the standard normal for finite degrees of freedom. Since \\(\\mathbb{E}[Z^{2}]=1, \\mathbb{E}[W]=m\\). The denominator \\(W/m\\) becomes close to one as m grows, explaining \\(t_{m} \\to \\mathcal{N}(0,1)\\). Likewise, as k grows\\(, F_{m,k}\\) approaches \\(\\chi ^{2}_{m}/m\\). Useful connection. Squaring \\(a t_{k}\\) variable gives \\(F_{1,k}\\). The numerator becomes \\(Z^{2}\\sim \\chi ^{2}_{1}\\) and the denominator remains \\(W/k\\). This later connects a two-sided \\(t\\) test for one restriction to an F test. Example. The 95th percentile of \\(\\chi ^{2}_{3}\\) is about 7.815, so the 95th percentile of \\(F_{3,\\infty }\\) is about \\(7.815/3=2.605. A 95\\)th percentile is an upper-tail cutoff leaving 5%, not the positive endpoint of every two-sided interval."}, {"file": "05-distributions.html", "id": "decision", "title": "Normal, χ², t & F · 4. Distribution names are conclusions, not labels", "text": "A statistic called a “t-statistic” need not have a Student \\(t\\) distribution. The exact \\(t\\) result requires a standard normal numerator and an independent chi-squared variance term. Without these facts, the name of the statistic cannot justify \\(t\\) critical values. The CLT offers a different, asymptotic justification discussed next. Check your understanding Question. If \\(Z\\sim \\mathcal{N}(0,1)\\) and \\(W=Z^{2}\\), does \\(Z/\\sqrt{W}\\) have \\(a t_{1}\\) distribution? Solution. No. The ratio equals the sign of \\(Z\\), taking only \\(\\pm 1\\). Independence in the \\(t\\) construction is essential. Sources: SW §2.4, pp. 82–88. The explicit conditional-mean formula and \\(t^{2}\\)–F identity are supporting derivations from the stated distribution properties."}, {"file": "06-sampling.html", "id": "sampling", "title": "Sampling, LLN & CLT · 1. Population, sample, and sampling distribution", "text": "Let \\(Y_{1},\\ldots ,Y_{n}\\) be independent and identically distributed (i.i.d.) with mean \\(\\mu\\) and variance \\(\\sigma ^{2}\\). “Identically distributed” means each observation has the same marginal distribution. “Independent” means one observation provides no information about the others. These are distinct restrictions. The population distribution describes an individual \\(Y\\). The empirical distribution describes observed values in one sample. The sampling distribution describes a statistic across repeated samples of size \\(n\\). Before observing the sample\\(, \\bar{Y}\\) is random; after observing it, its realized value is a number. 中文直觉：想象每次都重新抽 \\(n\\) 个人，每次只记录一个平均数。把很多次平均数画成直方图，才是“平均数的抽样分布”。把一次抽到的 \\(n\\) 个人画直方图，画的是另一件事。 Sampling clarification. The textbook works with an i.i.d. simple-random-sampling model. Sampling without replacement from a finite population introduces dependence. If the sampling fraction is substantial, finite-population corrections matter. Equal inclusion probabilities alone do not prove independence. Nonresponse, selective recruitment, and common shocks can also break the working model."}, {"file": "06-sampling.html", "id": "mean", "title": "Sampling, LLN & CLT · 2. Exact moments of the sample mean", "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}\\bar Y&=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^nY_i,\\\\ \\mathbb E[\\bar Y]&=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n\\mathbb E[Y_i]=\\mu,\\\\ \\operatorname{Var}(\\bar Y)&=\\frac1{n^2}\\sum_{i=1}^n\\operatorname{Var}(Y_i)=\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n},\\\\ \\operatorname{SD}(\\bar Y)&=\\frac\\sigma{\\sqrt n}.\\end{aligned}\\] The expectation calculation uses linearity. The variance calculation additionally uses zero cross-covariances, supplied by independence. These equalities are exact for every \\(n\\) under the assumptions; they do not require normality or a large sample. If the observations are normal\\(, \\bar{Y} \\sim \\mathcal{N}(\\mu ,\\sigma ^{2}/n)\\) exactly because a sum of independent normals is normal. For Bernoulli observations\\(, K=\\sum Y_{i}\\) has a binomial distribution and \\(\\bar{Y} =K/n\\): \\[P\\!\\left(\\bar Y=\\frac kn\\right)=\\binom nk p^k(1-p)^{n-k},\\quad k=0,\\ldots,n.\\] The binomial expression makes the small-sample distribution explicit. Its support is discrete, even though the large-sample approximation is continuous."}, {"file": "06-sampling.html", "id": "lln", "title": "Sampling, LLN & CLT · 3. Convergence in probability and the LLN", "text": "\\[\\begin{gathered}T_n\\xrightarrow{p}\\theta\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\\forall\\varepsilon>0,\\;P(|T_n-\\theta|>\\varepsilon)\\to0.\\\\ \\underbrace{Y_i\\text{ i.i.d.},\\;\\mathbb E[Y_i]=\\mu,\\;\\operatorname{Var}(Y_i)<\\infty}_{\\text{Sufficient LLN conditions}}\\\\ \\implies\\bar Y\\xrightarrow{p}\\mu.\\end{gathered}\\] Proof using Chebyshev'\\(s\\) inequality. For a variable \\(W\\) with finite variance\\(, \\Pr(|W-\\mathbb{E}[W]|\\ge \\varepsilon )\\le \\operatorname{Var}(W)/\\varepsilon ^{2}\\). Apply it to \\(\\bar{Y}\\) to obtain \\(\\Pr(|\\bar{Y} -\\mu |\\ge \\varepsilon )\\le \\sigma ^{2}/(n\\varepsilon ^{2})\\to 0\\). The bound need not be tight; its vanishing proves consistency. Consistency concerns arbitrarily small fixed neighborhoods of the target as \\(n\\) grows. It is not a claim that every additional observation improves a particular realized estimate. More general LLNs permit weaker conditions; finite variance is the sufficient version used in SW Key Concept 2.6."}, {"file": "06-sampling.html", "id": "clt", "title": "Sampling, LLN & CLT · 4. Convergence in distribution and the CLT", "text": "\\[\\begin{gathered}Z_n\\xrightarrow{d}Z\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad F_{Z_n}(z)\\to F_Z(z)\\\\ \\text{at every continuity point of }F_Z.\\\\ Y_i\\text{ i.i.d.},\\quad 0<\\sigma^2<\\infty\\\\ \\implies\\frac{\\sqrt n(\\bar Y-\\mu)}{\\sigma}\\xrightarrow{d}\\mathcal N(0,1).\\end{gathered}\\] Equivalently\\(, \\sqrt{n}(\\bar{Y} -\\mu ) \\xrightarrow{d} \\mathcal{N}(0,\\sigma ^{2})\\). The practical approximation is \\(\\bar{Y} \\approx \\mathcal{N}(\\mu ,\\sigma ^{2}/n)\\). The n-dependent approximation is not a claim that the unscaled mean converges to a fixed nondegenerate normal distribution. LLN CLT Object \\(\\bar{Y}\\) \\(\\sqrt{n}(\\bar{Y} -\\mu )/\\sigma\\) Question Does it concentrate at \\(\\mu\\)? What is the shape of normalized error? Use Consistency, plug-in estimates Approximate probabilities and inference 中文直觉：LLN 是把镜头固定，看平均数越来越挤在真值附近；CLT 是同时放大 \\(\\sqrt{n}\\) 倍，看缩小后的误差长什么样。两者完全兼容。分子趋于零、分母也趋于零的 t-statistic，不能直接当成零。"}, {"file": "06-sampling.html", "id": "accuracy", "title": "Sampling, LLN & CLT · 5. How large is large?", "text": "No universal \\(n=30\\) or \\(n=100\\) guarantee follows from the CLT. Accuracy depends on skewness, tails, event probabilities, and the desired tail approximation. Rare Bernoulli successes can require much larger \\(n\\). When a third moment exists, the standardized skewness of an i.i.d. sample mean is the individual skewness divided by \\(\\sqrt{n}\\). This helps explain why a skewed population can require more averaging. Dependence requires another CLT and often another variance. Infinite variance lies outside the classical result. The Cauchy example in the Aden notes is a useful boundary case: i.i.d. Cauchy averages remain Cauchy and do not acquire the concentration described above."}, {"file": "06-sampling.html", "id": "lab", "title": "Sampling, LLN & CLT · 6. Sampling laboratory", "text": "The local illustration below draws Bernoulli samples repeatedly. The top histogram shows sample means; the bottom shows the same means standardized using the true \\(\\sigma =\\sqrt{p(1-p)}\\). Change \\(n\\) while holding \\(p\\) fixed to distinguish concentration from shape. The simulation illustrates a theorem; it does not prove it. Sample size \\(n\\) 5 25 100 400 Success probability \\(p\\) 0.50 0.78 0.02 Run 3,000 samples Check your understanding Question. How much must \\(n\\) increase to halve \\(\\operatorname{SD}(\\bar{Y} )\\)? Does this halve \\(\\operatorname{SD}(Y)\\)? Solution. Quadruple \\(n\\). The population spread \\(\\sigma\\) is unchanged. Sources: SW §§2.5–2.6, pp. 89–99; Schellenberg Section 2, pp. 5–8; Aden notes §§3.1–3.4, pp. 10–14. Chebyshev proof and sampling caveat are supporting elaborations."}, {"file": "07-estimation.html", "id": "criteria", "title": "Estimators & Least Squares · 1. Estimators, bias, consistency, and efficiency", "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Bias}(\\hat\\theta)&=\\mathbb E[\\hat\\theta]-\\theta,\\\\ \\text{Unbiasedness: }&\\mathbb E[\\hat\\theta]=\\theta,\\\\ \\text{Consistency: }&\\hat\\theta_n\\xrightarrow{p}\\theta,\\\\ \\operatorname{MSE}(\\hat\\theta)&=\\mathbb E[(\\hat\\theta-\\theta)^2]\\\\&=\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat\\theta)+\\operatorname{Bias}(\\hat\\theta)^2.\\end{aligned}\\] An estimator is a function of the random sample. An estimate is its realized value. Bias and variance are finite-sample properties; consistency describes a sequence as \\(n\\) grows. Among unbiased estimators, a smaller variance means greater efficiency in the comparison used by this chapter. MSE provides an additional criterion when bias is allowed. Derivation of MSE. Decompose \\(\\hat{\\theta } -\\theta =(\\hat{\\theta } -\\mathbb{E}[\\hat{\\theta } ])+(\\mathbb{E}[\\hat{\\theta } ]-\\theta )\\). The cross term has expectation zero. A biased estimator may have lower MSE if the variance reduction outweighs squared bias. Estimator of \\(\\mu\\) under i.i.d. finite-variance sampling Bias Variance Consistent? \\(Y_{1}\\) 0 \\(\\sigma ^{2}\\) Generally no \\(\\bar{Y}\\) 0 \\(\\sigma ^{2}/n\\) Yes \\(\\bar{Y} +1/n\\) \\(1/n\\) \\(\\sigma ^{2}/n\\) Yes Alternating weights \\(.5/n, 1.5/n (\\)even \\(n\\)) 0 \\(1.25\\sigma ^{2}/n\\) Yes 中文直觉：无偏是“重复很多次，平均瞄准中心”；一致是“样本变大时，单次误差大概率变小”。只用第一个观察值一直可以无偏，却永远没有用上新增信息。"}, {"file": "07-estimation.html", "id": "blue", "title": "Estimators & Least Squares · 2. Why the sample mean is BLUE", "text": "Consider linear estimators \\(\\hat{\\mu } =\\sum w_{i}Y_{i}\\) with fixed, nonrandom weights. Unbiasedness for all \\(\\mu\\) requires \\(\\sum w_{i}=1\\). Under i.i.d. sampling with positive finite variance: \\[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat\\mu)&=\\sigma^2\\sum_i w_i^2,\\\\ \\sum_i\\left(w_i-\\frac1n\\right)^2&=\\sum_iw_i^2-\\frac1n\\ge0.\\end{aligned}\\] Therefore \\(\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat{\\mu } )\\ge \\sigma ^{2}/n\\), with equality precisely at \\(w_{i}=1/n\\). Thus \\(\\bar{Y}\\) is the Best Linear Unbiased Estimator within this class. “Best” is qualified by the class, the criterion, and the sampling assumptions. It does not compare every nonlinear or biased estimator under every loss function."}, {"file": "07-estimation.html", "id": "least-squares", "title": "Estimators & Least Squares · 3. Why the sample mean is the least-squares constant", "text": "Given observations, minimize \\(Q(m)=\\sum (Y_{i}-m)^{2}\\) over m. \\[Q'(m)=-2\\sum_i(Y_i-m)=0\\implies m=\\bar Y,\\qquad Q''(m)=2n>0.\\] The algebraic proof is even more revealing: \\[\\sum_i(Y_i-m)^2=\\sum_i(Y_i-\\bar Y)^2+n(\\bar Y-m)^2.\\] Expand \\(Y_{i}-m=(Y_{i}-\\bar{Y} )+(\\bar{Y} -m)\\); the cross term vanishes because \\(\\sum (Y_{i}-\\bar{Y} )=0\\). The second term is minimized at \\(m=\\bar{Y}\\). This is an algebraic fact for any data, without i.i.d. or normality. Its statistical performance as an estimate of a population mean requires separate assumptions. 中文直觉：最小二乘性质说“这组数据里哪个常数贴得最好”；BLUE 说“重复抽样时，哪条线性无偏规则最稳定”。一个是样本内优化，一个是抽样分布比较，不能混成一个理由。"}, {"file": "07-estimation.html", "id": "selection", "title": "Estimators & Least Squares · 4. More observations do not repair selection bias", "text": "If observations come from a selected population with mean \\(\\mu _{S}\\) rather than the target mean \\(\\mu\\), averaging may converge to \\(\\mu _{S}\\). Sampling unemployed people in parks during working hours illustrates this mismatch. A small standard error can coexist with a large error relative to the desired population. The polling box in SW §3.1 emphasizes representation and response behavior. Random sampling concerns whose outcomes enter the dataset; random assignment concerns who receives a treatment. These address different inferential problems."}, {"file": "07-estimation.html", "id": "nonlinear", "title": "Estimators & Least Squares · 5. Nonlinear transformations preserve some properties", "text": "\\(\\mathbb{E}[\\bar{Y} ^{2}]=\\mu ^{2}+\\sigma ^{2}/n\\), so \\(\\bar{Y} ^{2}\\) is generally biased for \\(\\mu ^{2}\\). Yet \\(\\bar{Y} ^{2} \\xrightarrow{p} \\mu ^{2}\\) by continuity of squaring and the LLN. Unbiasedness does not automatically survive a nonlinear transformation; consistency often does when continuity holds. Check your understanding Question. Does least-squares optimality of \\(\\bar{Y}\\) prove that a convenience sample estimates the national mean without bias? Solution. No. The minimization identity holds even for badly selected data. It does not establish representativeness. Sources: SW §3.1, pp. 112–117; Appendix 3.2, p. 153; Exercises 3.11 and 3.19; Aden notes p. 16. MSE is a supporting extension."}, {"file": "08-standard-errors.html", "id": "sample-variance", "title": "Variance Estimation & Standard Errors · 1. Sample variance and the \\(n-1\\) correction", "text": "\\[s^2=\\frac1{n-1}\\sum_i(Y_i-\\bar Y)^2,\\qquad s=\\sqrt{s^2},\\quad n\\ge2.\\] The residuals sum to zero, leaving \\(n-1\\) independently variable residual coordinates. Estimating the mean draws the residuals toward zero. The divisor \\(n-1\\) corrects the resulting downward bias under i.i.d. finite-variance sampling. \\[\\begin{aligned}\\sum_i(Y_i-\\bar Y)^2&=\\sum_i(Y_i-\\mu)^2-n(\\bar Y-\\mu)^2,\\\\ \\mathbb E\\!\\left[\\sum_i(Y_i-\\bar Y)^2\\right]&=n\\sigma^2-n\\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}=(n-1)\\sigma^2.\\end{aligned}\\] Thus \\(\\mathbb{E}[s^{2}]=\\sigma ^{2}\\). Dividing by \\(n\\) instead gives expectation \\((n-1)\\sigma ^{2}/n\\). The standard deviation \\(s\\) itself need not be unbiased for \\(\\sigma\\): the square root is nonlinear."}, {"file": "08-standard-errors.html", "id": "consistency", "title": "Variance Estimation & Standard Errors · 2. Why sample variance is consistent", "text": "\\[s^2=\\frac n{n-1}\\left(\\frac1n\\sum_iY_i^2-\\bar Y^2\\right)\\xrightarrow{p}\\mathbb E[Y^2]-\\mu^2=\\sigma^2.\\] SW Appendix 3.3 assumes i.i.d. observations and \\(\\mathbb{E}[Y^{4}]<\\infty\\). Under these sufficient conditions, the finite-variance LLN applies to \\(Y^{2}\\), while the LLN for \\(Y\\) and continuity give \\(\\bar{Y} ^{2}\\xrightarrow{p} \\mu ^{2}\\). The prefactor approaches one. A stronger LLN can prove this consistency using only a finite second moment, but that is a weaker-assumption extension, not the particular proof used in the chapter."}, {"file": "08-standard-errors.html", "id": "se", "title": "Variance Estimation & Standard Errors · 3. Three different measures of spread", "text": "Quantity Meaning Formula Population SD Spread of individual outcomes \\(\\sigma\\) Sampling SD of the mean Spread of \\(\\bar{Y}\\) across repeated samples \\(\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}\\) Estimated standard error Estimate of that sampling SD \\(\\operatorname{SE}(\\bar{Y} )=s/\\sqrt{n}\\) 中文直觉：收入差异很大，是个人之间的分散程度；平均收入估得准不准，是平均数在重复抽样中的波动。样本多会让后者变小，不会让现实中的收入差异自动消失。"}, {"file": "08-standard-errors.html", "id": "slutsky", "title": "Variance Estimation & Standard Errors · 4. Continuous mapping and Slutsky'\\(s\\) theorem", "text": "Supporting theorem connection • Aden notes §3.3 Continuous mapping theorem, constant-limit form. If \\(T_{n}\\xrightarrow{p} \\theta\\) and g is continuous at \\(\\theta\\), then \\(g(T_{n})\\xrightarrow{p} g(\\theta )\\). Thus \\(s^{2}\\xrightarrow{p} \\sigma ^{2}>0\\) implies \\(s\\xrightarrow{p} \\sigma\\) and \\(\\sigma /s\\xrightarrow{p} 1\\). Slutsky'\\(s\\) theorem. If \\(A_{n}\\xrightarrow{d} A\\) and \\(B_{n}\\xrightarrow{p} b\\) for a constant b, then \\(A_{n}+B_{n}\\xrightarrow{d} A+b\\) and \\(A_{n}B_{n}\\xrightarrow{d}\\)bA; division is valid when \\(b\\ne 0\\). Independence of \\(A_{n}\\) and \\(B_{n}\\) is not required. \\[\\frac{\\sqrt n(\\bar Y-\\mu)}s=\\frac{\\sqrt n(\\bar Y-\\mu)}\\sigma\\cdot\\frac\\sigma s\\xrightarrow{d}\\mathcal N(0,1).\\] This is studentization : replace unknown sampling scale by a consistent estimate. It justifies the approximate standard normal distribution of the t-statistic under a true null. Because both SE and the true sampling SD shrink toward zero, ratio consistency \\(\\operatorname{SE}/(\\sigma /\\sqrt{n})\\xrightarrow{p} 1\\) is the useful property; merely showing both approach zero would not suffice."}, {"file": "08-standard-errors.html", "id": "proportions", "title": "Variance Estimation & Standard Errors · 5. Sample proportions and a finite-sample convention", "text": "For Bernoulli observations\\(, \\hat{p} =\\bar{Y}\\) and \\(\\sum (Y_{i}-\\hat{p} )^{2}=n\\hat{p} (1-\\hat{p} )\\). Consequently: \\[\\begin{aligned}s^2&=\\frac n{n-1}\\hat p(1-\\hat p),\\\\ \\frac s{\\sqrt n}&=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)}{n-1}},\\\\ \\operatorname{SE}_{\\text{plug-in}}(\\hat p)&=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\hat p(1-\\hat p)}n}.\\end{aligned}\\] The last expression directly plugs \\(\\hat{p}\\) into the Bernoulli population variance and is the usual large-sample proportion formula used in the text'\\(s\\) discussion. It is asymptotically equivalent to \\(s/\\sqrt{n}\\), but not exactly equal at finite \\(n\\). Keep the convention explicit when checking numerical work. For testing \\(H_{0}\\):\\(p=p_{0}, a\\) null-based scale \\(\\sqrt{p_{0}(1-p_{0})/n}\\) yields a score-type statistic. This differs from the plug-in Wald statistic. Near \\(p=0\\) or 1 or with very few successes, neither a naive Wald interval nor a generic normal rule should be assumed reliable. Check your understanding Question. Is \\(\\operatorname{SE}(\\bar{Y} )=s/n\\)? Does Slutsky require the sample mean and sample variance to be independent? Solution. \\(\\operatorname{SE}=s/\\sqrt{n}\\). Independence is unnecessary for Slutsky when the scale ratio converges to a constant. Sources: SW §3.2, pp. 120–123; Appendix 3.3, p. 154; Exercise 3.18; Aden notes pp. 12–14. The Bernoulli divisor distinction explicitly reconciles the sample-variance definition and large-sample plug-in convention."}, {"file": "09-inference.html", "id": "null", "title": "Tests & Confidence Intervals · 1. State the target and the hypotheses", "text": "For a population mean \\(\\mu , a\\) two-sided test compares \\(H_{0}\\):\\(\\mu =\\mu _{0}\\) with \\(H_{1}\\):\\(\\mu \\ne \\mu _{0}\\). Upper- and lower-sided alternatives are \\(\\mu >\\mu _{0}\\) and \\(\\mu <\\mu _{0}\\). Choose the direction before looking at the result. A test either rejects \\(H_{0}\\) or fails to reject it; failure to reject does not establish equality. \\[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Known }\\sigma:\\quad Z&=\\frac{\\bar Y-\\mu_0}{\\sigma/\\sqrt n},\\\\ \\text{Unknown }\\sigma:\\quad t&=\\frac{\\bar Y-\\mu_0}{\\operatorname{SE}(\\bar Y)},\\\\ \\operatorname{SE}(\\bar Y)&=\\frac s{\\sqrt n}.\\end{aligned}\\] Under the true null, the large-sample reference distribution is \\(\\mathcal{N}(0,1)\\), using the CLT and, when necessary, Slutsky. With normal observations and known \\(\\sigma\\), the \\(Z\\) distribution is exact for any \\(n\\). The exact small-sample unknown\\(-\\sigma\\) case is in Module 11."}, {"file": "09-inference.html", "id": "pvalue", "title": "Tests & Confidence Intervals · 2. What a p-value means", "text": "A p-value is a null-model probability of a statistic at least as adverse to \\(H_{0}\\) as the observed statistic, using the preselected direction of the test. Alternative Approximate p-value 5% rejection rule \\(\\mu \\ne \\mu _{0}\\) \\(2[1-\\Phi (|t_{\\text{obs}}|)]\\) \\(|t_{\\text{obs}}|>1.96\\) \\(\\mu >\\mu _{0}\\) \\(1-\\Phi (t_{\\text{obs}})\\) \\(t_{\\text{obs}}>1.645\\) \\(\\mu <\\mu _{0}\\) \\(\\Phi (t_{\\text{obs}})\\) \\(t_{\\text{obs}}<-1.645\\) For a two-sided test, distance is measured in both tails. For an upper-sided test, a large negative statistic gives a large p-value. A p-value is not \\(\\Pr(H_{0}\\) is true|data), the probability that chance “caused” the result, or the probability of the exact observed sample. 中文直觉：先暂时把原假设当真，再问“如果按这套模型重复抽样，会有多经常出现这么极端或更极端的统计量？”这是一道条件概率问题，不是在给原假设本身分配真假概率。"}, {"file": "09-inference.html", "id": "errors", "title": "Tests & Confidence Intervals · 3. Significance, size, power, and errors", "text": "Truth / decision Reject \\(H_{0}\\) Fail to reject \\(H_{0}\\) \\(H_{0}\\) true Type I error Correct nonrejection \\(H_{0}\\) false Correct rejection Type II error The significance level \\(\\alpha\\) is the chosen Type I error target. The size is the actual null rejection probability (or its supremum over a composite null). Approximation errors can make actual size differ from nominal \\(\\alpha\\). The power at an alternative is the probability of rejection there; it equals one minus its Type II error probability. Critical values define a rejection region. Lowering \\(\\alpha\\) while retaining the same test family raises the cutoff and reduces power. Larger \\(n\\) typically raises power against a fixed nonzero effect by reducing standard error. Statistical significance does not measure economic importance: a tiny effect can be precisely estimated, and an important effect can be imprecisely estimated. \\[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Reject if }\\bar Y&>\\mu_0+z_{1-\\alpha}\\frac\\sigma{\\sqrt n},\\\\ \\operatorname{Power}(\\mu_1)&=1-\\Phi\\!\\left[z_{1-\\alpha}-\\frac{(\\mu_1-\\mu_0)\\sqrt n}{\\sigma}\\right].\\end{aligned}\\] This formula is exact for normal observations and approximate under a suitable CLT. It displays the roles of the effect, noise, sample size, and cutoff. With many true nulls, repeated testing can create false positives; for m independent tests at \\(\\alpha\\), the probability of at least one false rejection is \\(1-(1-\\alpha )\\)ᵐ."}, {"file": "09-inference.html", "id": "worked", "title": "Tests & Confidence Intervals · 4. Worked inference from start to finish", "text": "Use the textbook'\\(s\\) hypothetical wage sample: \\(n=200, \\bar{Y} =22.64, s=18.14\\), and \\(H_{0}\\):\\(\\mu =20\\) versus a two-sided alternative. Then \\(\\operatorname{SE}=18.14/\\sqrt{200}\\approx 1.2827, t\\approx 2.0582\\), and \\(p\\approx .0396\\). Reject at 5%, but not at 1%. The estimated difference is 2.64 dollars per hour; report it with uncertainty, not just a rejection label."}, {"file": "09-inference.html", "id": "interval", "title": "Tests & Confidence Intervals · 5. Confidence intervals by test inversion", "text": "Collect all null values \\(\\mu _{0}\\) that the two-sided test does not reject: \\[\\begin{gathered}\\left|\\frac{\\bar Y-\\mu_0}{\\operatorname{SE}}\\right|\\le z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\\\ \\Longleftrightarrow\\quad \\bar Y-z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\operatorname{SE}\\le\\mu_0\\le\\bar Y+z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\operatorname{SE},\\\\ \\operatorname{CI}_{1-\\alpha}=\\left[\\bar Y-z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\operatorname{SE},\\;\\bar Y+z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\operatorname{SE}\\right].\\end{gathered}\\] The 95% wage interval is approximately [20.126,25.154]. Since 20 lies outside it, the matching two-sided 5% test rejects. Test–interval duality requires the same estimator, standard error, reference distribution, and sidedness. Mixing conventions can break the apparent agreement. Coverage: \\(P_\\mu\\{\\mu\\in\\operatorname{CI}(\\text{data})\\}\\approx1-\\alpha\\) across repeated samples. The interval endpoints are random; \\(\\mu\\) is fixed in this frequentist formulation. After observing the data, the particular interval either contains \\(\\mu\\) or does not. A 95% interval for the mean is not an interval containing 95% of individual outcomes. 中文直觉：置信度是“造区间的方法”在重复抽样中的命中率。把很多次实验得到的区间摆在一起，约 95% 会覆盖同一个真值。更高置信度需要更宽的网，不意味着这次估计更精确。"}, {"file": "09-inference.html", "id": "precision", "title": "Tests & Confidence Intervals · 6. Precision and sample-size planning", "text": "The margin of error is \\(z\\cdot \\operatorname{SE}\\). At fixed confidence level and population spread, halving the margin requires four times as many independent observations. For a Bernoulli proportion\\(, p(1-p)\\le 1/4\\) gives a conservative normal-approximation planning formula: \\[n\\ge\\frac{z_{1-\\alpha/2}^2}{4e^2}.\\] where e is the desired half-width, and \\(n\\) is rounded up. For 95% and \\(e=.005\\), using \\(z=1.96\\) gives \\(n\\ge 38,416\\). This controls the planned approximate sampling margin under the design; it does not eliminate selection bias or guarantee realized coverage under every small-sample configuration. A one-sided 95% lower confidence set is [\\(\\bar{Y} -1.645SE,\\infty\\)); an upper confidence set is (\\(-\\infty ,\\bar{Y} +1.645SE\\)]. These invert the corresponding one-sided tests. Check your understanding Question. A two-sided p-value for \\(\\mu _{0}\\) is .07. Is \\(\\mu _{0}\\) in the matching 90% and 95% intervals? Solution. It is excluded from the 90% interval, since .07<.10; included in the 95% interval, since .07>.05. Sources: SW §§3.2–3.3, pp. 117–128; Exercises 3.5–3.6 and 3.9. The explicit power and familywise-error formulas are supporting derivations."}, {"file": "10-comparisons-causality.html", "id": "difference", "title": "Comparisons & Causal Effects · 1. Comparing two independent population means", "text": "Let the target be \\(\\delta =\\mu _{A}-\\mu _{B}\\). Within each group assume i.i.d. sampling, and assume the two samples are independent. Then \\(\\hat{\\delta } =\\bar{Y} _{A}-\\bar{Y} _{B}\\) is unbiased, and: \\[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat\\delta)&=\\frac{\\sigma_A^2}{n_A}+\\frac{\\sigma_B^2}{n_B},\\\\ \\operatorname{SE}(\\hat\\delta)&=\\sqrt{\\frac{s_A^2}{n_A}+\\frac{s_B^2}{n_B}},\\\\ t&=\\frac{\\hat\\delta-\\delta_0}{\\operatorname{SE}(\\hat\\delta)},\\\\ \\operatorname{CI}&=\\hat\\delta\\pm z_{1-\\alpha/2}\\operatorname{SE}(\\hat\\delta).\\end{aligned}\\] The variances add because \\(\\operatorname{Var}(U-V)=\\operatorname{Var}(U)+\\operatorname{Var}(V)-2Cov(U,V)\\) and the samples are independent. Different means do not require equal variances. With both group sizes sufficiently large and appropriate finite moments, the centered, studentized difference is approximately \\(\\mathcal{N}(0,1)\\). For two proportions, replace the group variance by \\(\\hat{p} _{g}(1-\\hat{p} _{g})\\) in the large-sample plug-in convention. A difference of proportions is measured in percentage points, not automatically percent."}, {"file": "10-comparisons-causality.html", "id": "earnings", "title": "Comparisons & Causal Effects · 2. Historical earnings example and changing populations", "text": "SW Table 3.1 reports, for 2012 college-educated full-time workers aged 25–34: men \\(n=2004\\), mean 25.30, SD 12.09; women \\(n=1951\\), mean 21.50, SD 9.99. The estimated gap is 3.80 dollars per hour\\(. \\operatorname{SE}\\approx .35227\\) and the 95% interval is approximately [3.110,4.490]. The 1992 gap in 2012 dollars is \\(24.83-21.39=3.44\\). Under the exercise'\\(s\\) assumption of independent samples across years, the change in the gap is .36 and its variance is the sum of the four group-mean variances. Using the reported sample sizes and SDs gives \\(\\operatorname{SE}\\approx .49940\\) and a 95% interval of approximately \\([-.619,1.339]\\). Evidence of a positive gap in each year does not establish evidence of an increase between years. Table 2.4 covers a broader age population, so its means need not equal Table 3.1'\\(s\\) means. Nominal earnings across years must be converted to a common price level for purchasing-power comparisons: real earnings in base-year dollars = nominal earnings \\(\\times\\) CPI\\(_{\\text{base}}/\\)CPI\\(_{\\text{year}}\\). The original table'\\(s\\) historical CPI adjustment is part of its measurement definition. 中文直觉：A 年显著、B 年也显著，不代表 A 到 B 的变化显著。必须直接估计“差的变化”并计算它的标准误。另外先对齐年龄范围、全职定义和价格单位，否则是在比较不同问题。"}, {"file": "10-comparisons-causality.html", "id": "paired", "title": "Comparisons & Causal Effects · 3. Paired observations are a different design", "text": "When the same person is measured before and after a course, the two measurements are dependent. Define \\(D_{i}=Y_{i,after}-Y_{i,before}\\) and analyze the independent pairs through their differences: \\[\\begin{aligned}\\hat\\delta&=\\bar D,\\qquad\\operatorname{SE}(\\bar D)=\\frac{s_D}{\\sqrt n},\\\\ \\operatorname{Var}(D_i)&=\\operatorname{Var}(Y_{\\text{after}})+\\operatorname{Var}(Y_{\\text{before}})\\\\&\\quad-2\\operatorname{Cov}(Y_{\\text{after}},Y_{\\text{before}}).\\end{aligned}\\] A before–after gain can reflect a treatment, practice, aging, or other time changes. Pairing addresses a variance calculation; it does not by itself identify a causal treatment effect."}, {"file": "10-comparisons-causality.html", "id": "experiment", "title": "Comparisons & Causal Effects · 4. Randomized experiments and conditional means", "text": "SW §3.5 defines a treatment effect using an ideal randomized experiment. With binary treatment D, the difference \\(\\mathbb{E}[Y|D=1]-\\mathbb{E}[Y|D=0]\\) has a causal interpretation when treatment assignment makes the groups comparable in their potential outcomes. Supporting notation • potential outcomes Let Y(1) and Y(0) be the outcomes under treatment and control, and \\(Y=D Y(1)+(1-D)Y(0)\\). Assume well-defined treatments, no interference between units, positive probability of either assignment, and random assignment D independent of (Y(1),Y(0)). Then: \\[\\begin{aligned}\\mathbb E[Y\\mid D=1]-\\mathbb E[Y\\mid D=0]&=\\mathbb E[Y(1)]-\\mathbb E[Y(0)]\\\\&=\\mathbb E[Y(1)-Y(0)].\\end{aligned}\\] Derivation. Among treated units the observed outcome is Y(1); independence removes the conditioning on D. Apply the same reasoning to control units and subtract. The sample difference estimates the average treatment effect for the experimental population. The standard-error formula must match the sampling and assignment design; the chapter uses its independent-group framework. Random sampling helps generalize to a target population. Random assignment helps identify a treatment effect within the recruited population. An experiment can have good random assignment but limited external validity. Noncompliance, selective attrition, or spillovers can require additional analysis."}, {"file": "10-comparisons-causality.html", "id": "selection-bias", "title": "Comparisons & Causal Effects · 5. Why observational differences are not automatically causal", "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}&\\mathbb E[Y\\mid D=1]-\\mathbb E[Y\\mid D=0]\\\\&\\quad=\\underbrace{\\mathbb E[Y(1)-Y(0)\\mid D=1]}_{\\text{Average treatment effect on the treated}}\\\\&\\qquad+\\underbrace{\\mathbb E[Y(0)\\mid D=1]-\\mathbb E[Y(0)\\mid D=0]}_{\\text{Selection bias}}.\\end{aligned}\\] The first term is the average treatment effect on the treated; the bracketed term is selection bias. Observed education or gender earnings differences do not, by themselves, isolate discrimination, education effects, skills, occupation selection, or experience. A small sampling p-value does not remove the bracketed term. 中文直觉：处理组和对照组差了多少，包含“处理改变了多少”，也可能包含“这两组原本就差多少”。随机分配的作用是让第二部分在设计上消失，而不是让 \\(t\\) 值变得更大。 Natural or quasi-experiments seek circumstances that plausibly assign exposure as if randomly, under explicit assumptions. They do not become valid merely because researchers call them experiments. The voter-turnout box in SW p. 136 illustrates randomized outreach; its identification lesson is the controlled comparison, not a comparison with an earlier national turnout rate. Check your understanding Question. Students volunteer for a prep course and score higher afterward. Which two issues must be separated? Solution. Statistical uncertainty in the observed gain and identification of the course'\\(s\\) effect. Volunteer selection and practice effects are not removed by a precise paired mean. Sources: SW §§3.4–3.5, pp. 128–133; voter-turnout box p. 136; Exercises 3.15–3.17; Appendix 3.1. Potential-outcome notation and the selection decomposition are explicit extensions."}, {"file": "11-small-samples.html", "id": "exact", "title": "Small Samples & Student t · 1. Exact one-sample Student \\(t\\) inference", "text": "Assume \\(Y_{1},\\ldots ,Y_{n}\\) are \\(i.i.d. \\mathcal{N}(\\mu ,\\sigma ^{2})\\), with \\(\\sigma ^{2}>0\\) and \\(n\\ge 2\\). Normal sampling gives three facts: \\[\\begin{aligned}Z&=\\frac{\\sqrt n(\\bar Y-\\mu)}\\sigma\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1),\\\\ W&=\\frac{(n-1)s^2}{\\sigma^2}\\sim\\chi^2_{n-1},\\\\ Z&\\perp\\!\\!\\!\\perp W.\\end{aligned}\\] These facts imply \\((\\bar{Y} -\\mu )/(s/\\sqrt{n})=Z/\\sqrt{W/(n-1)}\\sim t_{n-1}\\). Under \\(H_{0}\\), replace \\(\\mu\\) by \\(\\mu _{0}\\). Independence and the chi-squared law are special normal-sample results, not consequences of unbiasedness of \\(s^{2}\\). The ratio derivation is supplied here; proving those normal-sample distribution facts belongs to the later distribution theory referenced by SW. \\[\\operatorname{CI}_{1-\\alpha}=\\bar Y\\pm t_{n-1,\\,1-\\alpha/2}\\frac s{\\sqrt n}.\\] For \\(n=20\\) the 95% critical value is about 2.093, instead of 1.96. A statistic 2.15 rejects at 5% under the exact normal-sample model. If the population is strongly nonnormal, neither the label “t-statistic” nor \\(n=20\\) justifies that exact result."}, {"file": "11-small-samples.html", "id": "pooled", "title": "Small Samples & Student t · 2. Exact pooled two-sample inference", "text": "Assume two independent samples, each i.i.d. normal, with respective means \\(\\mu _{A}, \\mu _{B}\\) and a common variance \\(\\sigma ^{2}\\). Define: \\[\\begin{aligned}s_p^2&=\\frac{(n_A-1)s_A^2+(n_B-1)s_B^2}{n_A+n_B-2},\\\\ \\operatorname{SE}_p&=s_p\\sqrt{\\frac1{n_A}+\\frac1{n_B}},\\\\ t_p&=\\frac{(\\bar Y_A-\\bar Y_B)-\\delta_0}{\\operatorname{SE}_p}\\\\&\\sim t_{n_A+n_B-2}\\quad\\text{under }H_0.\\end{aligned}\\] Each group'\\(s\\) deviations must be centered around its own group mean. The supplied PDF'\\(s\\) equation (3.23) prints the second group'\\(s\\) deviations around the first group'\\(s\\) mean; the notes use the mathematically correct within-group expression above and record this source error."}, {"file": "11-small-samples.html", "id": "equal-n", "title": "Small Samples & Student t · 3. Equal sample sizes are not equal population variances", "text": "If \\(n_{A}=n_{B}=m\\), the pooled and unpooled standard errors are algebraically equal: \\[\\begin{aligned}s_p^2&=\\frac{s_A^2+s_B^2}{2},\\\\ \\operatorname{SE}_p^2&=\\frac{s_A^2+s_B^2}{m}=\\operatorname{SE}_{\\text{unpooled}}^2.\\end{aligned}\\] But unequal population variances still generally prevent the pooled denominator from being the required common-scale chi-squared variable. Equal group sizes therefore do not alone imply an exact \\(t_{2m-2}\\) distribution. This distinction resolves potentially misleading wording in SW §3.6."}, {"file": "11-small-samples.html", "id": "welch", "title": "Small Samples & Student t · 4. Unequal variances and Welch'\\(s\\) approximation", "text": "Optional extension beyond SW'\\(s\\) large-sample treatment For independent normal groups with possibly unequal variances, the unpooled statistic is not generally exactly Student \\(t\\). Welch'\\(s\\) method approximates its degrees of freedom by: \\[\\hat\\nu=\\frac{\\left(s_A^2/n_A+s_B^2/n_B\\right)^2}{\\dfrac{(s_A^2/n_A)^2}{n_A-1}+\\dfrac{(s_B^2/n_B)^2}{n_B-1}}.\\] This is an approximation, not a general exact distribution theorem. For Econ 141'\\(s\\) main large-sample route, use the unpooled SE and the justified normal approximation. Dependence, severe skewness, or rare outcomes require attention to the actual design and distribution; changing degrees of freedom alone is not a universal repair. Setting Reference distribution One mean, i.i.d. normal, unknown variance Exact \\(t_{n-1}\\) One mean, general i.i.d. finite moments, large \\(n\\) Asymptotic \\(\\mathcal{N}(0,1)\\) Two normal independent groups, equal variances, pooled SE Exact \\(t_{n§SUB8§+n§SUB9§-2}\\) Two independent groups, unequal variances, both large Asymptotic \\(\\mathcal{N}(0,1)\\), unpooled SE Two normal groups, unequal variances, smaller samples Welch approximation; generally not exact \\(t\\) Check your understanding Question. Both groups have 12 observations and very different variances. Does equal \\(n\\) establish an exact pooled \\(t\\) distribution? Solution. No. It equates the two SE formulas, not the required distribution of the denominator. Sources: SW §3.6, pp. 133–137; Exercise 3.21. Welch degrees of freedom are an optional supporting method. See the source log for equation (3.23) and its correction."}, {"file": "12-regression-connections.html", "id": "scatterplots", "title": "Correlation → Regression · 1. Scatterplots before summary coefficients", "text": "A scatterplot places each observed pair \\((X_{i},Y_{i})\\) at a point. Inspect direction, curvature, spread, clusters, and unusual observations. A clear U-shaped relationship can have zero correlation. A high correlation indicates closeness to a straight line, not a steep slope. The original age–earnings example uses 200 information-industry managers. Its positive but imperfect association is descriptive; age alone does not determine earnings. The example does not establish an effect of aging while holding all other factors fixed."}, {"file": "12-regression-connections.html", "id": "sample-moments", "title": "Correlation → Regression · 2. Sample covariance and correlation", "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}s_{XY}&=\\frac1{n-1}\\sum_i(X_i-\\bar X)(Y_i-\\bar Y),\\\\ r_{XY}&=\\frac{s_{XY}}{s_Xs_Y},\\qquad |r_{XY}|\\le1.\\end{aligned}\\] Nonzero sample standard deviations are required. Adding constants leaves covariance unchanged; rescaling \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\) by b and c multiplies covariance by bc. Positive unit changes leave correlation unchanged; one negative rescaling reverses its sign. SW reports \\(s_{\\text{Age}}=9.07, s_{\\text{Earnings}}=14.37\\), and \\(s_{Age,Earnings}=33.16\\). The correlation is \\(33.16/(9.07\\cdot 14.37)\\approx .2544\\). In cents, both the earnings SD and covariance multiply by 100 and the correlation stays the same. Correlation .25 does not mean that 25% of outcomes are predicted correctly."}, {"file": "12-regression-connections.html", "id": "consistency", "title": "Correlation → Regression · 3. Consistency is a sample-moment argument", "text": "\\[\\begin{aligned}s_{XY}&=\\frac n{n-1}\\left(\\frac1n\\sum_iX_iY_i-\\bar X\\bar Y\\right)\\\\&\\xrightarrow{p}\\mathbb E[XY]-\\mathbb E[X]\\mathbb E[Y].\\end{aligned}\\] For the textbook proof route, assume i.i.d. pairs and finite fourth moments of \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\). Cauchy–Schwarz gives \\(\\mathbb{E}[X^{2}Y^{2}]\\le \\sqrt{\\mathbb{E}[X^{4}]\\mathbb{E}[Y^{4}]}<\\infty\\), permitting the finite-variance LLN for XY. Combine it with LLNs for \\(X\\) and \\(Y\\). Consistent sample variances and continuous mapping then imply \\(r_{XY}\\xrightarrow{p} \\rho _{XY}\\), provided population variances are positive."}, {"file": "12-regression-connections.html", "id": "projection", "title": "Correlation → Regression · 4. From a constant predictor to a line", "text": "Forward connection • regression Module 7 chose a constant to minimize squared errors. Now choose a and b to minimize \\(\\mathbb{E}[(Y-a-\\)bX\\()^{2}\\)]. With finite second moments and \\(\\operatorname{Var}(X)>0\\), the first-order conditions give: \\[\\begin{aligned}\\mathbb E[Y-a-bX]&=0,\\\\ \\mathbb E[X(Y-a-bX)]&=0,\\\\ b&=\\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)}{\\operatorname{Var}(X)},\\\\ a&=\\mathbb E[Y]-b\\mathbb E[X].\\end{aligned}\\] Replacing population moments by sample moments yields the simple OLS slope \\(\\hat{b} =s_{XY}/s_{X}^{2}\\) and intercept â\\(=\\bar{Y} -\\hat{b} \\bar{X} . A\\) positive sample variance is needed. Thus regression estimation is a direct continuation of Chapter 3'\\(s\\) sample covariance. For a binary \\(X\\), the fitted group values equal their sample means, and \\(\\hat{b} =\\bar{Y} _{1}-\\bar{Y} _{0}\\). The linear projection residual is orthogonal to 1 and \\(X\\) by construction. It need not have \\(\\mathbb{E}[u\\mid X]=0\\) unless the conditional mean is correctly represented by the line. Projection orthogonality and causal exogeneity are different statements."}, {"file": "12-regression-connections.html", "id": "identification", "title": "Correlation → Regression · 5. Covariance ratios, omitted factors, and IV", "text": "Schellenberg Section 2 considers \\(Y=a+\\)bX\\(+U\\). Covariance algebra gives: \\[\\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)}{\\operatorname{Var}(X)}=b+\\frac{\\operatorname{Cov}(X,U)}{\\operatorname{Var}(X)}.\\] If \\(\\operatorname{Cov}(X,U)\\ne 0\\), the observed covariance ratio differs from the structural coefficient b. If an instrument \\(Z\\) satisfies \\(\\operatorname{Cov}(Z,U)=0\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Cov}(Z,X)\\ne 0\\), then \\(\\operatorname{Cov}(Z,Y)/\\operatorname{Cov}(Z,X)=b\\). These are identification restrictions, not automatic consequences of observing a third variable. 中文直觉：回归斜率从“\\(X\\) 与 \\(Y\\) 怎么一起动”出发。如果遗漏因素 U 也跟 \\(X\\) 一起动，斜率就把两条路径混在一起。工具变量的想法是找一个只通过 \\(X\\) 带来变化的来源；公式简单，条件很强。"}, {"file": "12-regression-connections.html", "id": "asymptotic", "title": "Correlation → Regression · 6. The same probability engine behind OLS", "text": "For i.i.d. data satisfying \\(Y=\\beta _{0}+\\beta _{1}X+u\\) and \\(\\mathbb{E}[u\\mid X]=0\\), let \\(Q=\\operatorname{Var}(X)>0. A\\) sufficient moment condition for the following argument is \\(\\mathbb{E}[X^{4}]<\\infty\\) and \\(\\mathbb{E}[u^{4}]<\\infty\\), which ensures a finite second moment of the score \\((X-\\mathbb{E}[X])u\\): \\[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt n(\\hat b-\\beta_1)&=\\left\\{\\frac1n\\sum_i(X_i-\\bar X)^2\\right\\}^{-1}\\\\&\\quad\\cdot\\left\\{\\frac1{\\sqrt n}\\sum_i(X_i-\\bar X)u_i\\right\\},\\\\ \\text{LLN: denominator}&\\xrightarrow{p}Q,\\\\ \\text{CLT: leading numerator}&\\xrightarrow{d}\\mathcal N(0,\\Omega),\\\\ \\Omega&=\\mathbb E[(X-\\mathbb E[X])^2u^2],\\\\ \\text{Slutsky: }\\sqrt n(\\hat b-\\beta_1)&\\xrightarrow{d}\\mathcal N\\!\\left(0,\\frac\\Omega{Q^2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\\] Replacing \\(\\bar{X}\\) by \\(\\mathbb{E}[X]\\) in the numerator changes it by \\(\\sqrt{n}(\\bar{X} -\\mathbb{E}[X])\\)ū, which vanishes in probability under these conditions. The asymptotic variance allows the conditional error variance to vary with \\(X\\). Estimating it is the next step toward heteroskedasticity-robust standard errors. The matrix version in Aden notes p. 23 uses \\(Q=\\mathbb{E}[\\)xx′] and \\(\\Omega =\\mathbb{E}[u^{2}\\)xx′], giving \\(Q^{-1}\\Omega Q^{-1}\\). The architecture is unchanged: consistent denominator, approximately normal average in the numerator, and studentization. This extension is a preview rather than an additional prerequisite for Chapters 2–3. Check your understanding Question. Does a slope \\(\\hat{b} =0\\) establish that \\(Y\\) is unrelated to \\(X\\)? Solution. No. It establishes zero estimated linear slope when the sample variance is positive. A nonlinear conditional mean can still vary with \\(X\\). Sources: SW §3.7, pp. 137–141, Exercise 3.20; Schellenberg Section 2, pp. 2–5; Aden notes pp. 12–13 and 23. Regression derivations are forward extensions."}, {"file": "13-bootstrap-papers.html", "id": "bootstrap", "title": "Bootstrap & Paper Connections · 1. A local model of the sampling process", "text": "Let F be the unknown population distribution and \\(\\hat{F} _{n}(y)=n^{-1}\\sum 1{Y_{i}\\le y}\\) the empirical CDF. The nonparametric bootstrap replaces draws from F by draws from \\(\\hat{F} _{n}\\), conditional on the observed data. Draw \\(n\\) observations with replacement from the original \\(n\\) observations. Compute the estimator \\(\\hat{\\theta } *\\) in that bootstrap sample. Repeat B times to approximate its conditional distribution. Use an appropriate centered or studentized statistic to approximate sampling error. \\[\\begin{aligned}\\mathbb E^*[\\bar Y^*]&=\\bar Y,\\\\ \\operatorname{Var}^*(\\bar Y^*)&=\\frac1{n^2}\\sum_i(Y_i-\\bar Y)^2=\\frac{n-1}{n}\\frac{s^2}{n}.\\end{aligned}\\] The star indicates probability or expectation conditional on the observed sample. The empirical distribution has variance \\(n^{-1}\\sum (Y_{i}-\\bar{Y} )^{2}\\), and averaging \\(n\\) independent draws from it divides that variance by \\(n\\). Thus the bootstrap reproduces the familiar mean SE up to the same finite-sample divisor issue discussed earlier. For a studentized bootstrap, compute \\(T*=(\\hat{\\theta } *-\\hat{\\theta } )/\\operatorname{SE}*\\). If its bootstrap quantiles are \\(q*_{\\alpha /2}\\) and \\(q*_{1-\\alpha /2}\\), inversion gives \\([\\hat{\\theta } -q*_{1-\\alpha /2}\\operatorname{SE}, \\hat{\\theta } -q*_{\\alpha /2}\\operatorname{SE}]\\). This differs from simply taking percentiles of \\(\\hat{\\theta } *\\) or using 1.96 times a bootstrap SE. 中文直觉：Monte Carlo 是“知道一个总体模型，反复从它抽样”；bootstrap 是“只有手里的样本，把它当成临时总体再抽样”。临时总体能否模仿真正的抽样误差，需要证明，不能由“重复次数很多”保证。"}, {"file": "13-bootstrap-papers.html", "id": "failure", "title": "Bootstrap & Paper Connections · 2. Why resampling is not a universal cure", "text": "Consider i.i.d. Uniform\\([0,\\theta ]\\) data and the sample maximum \\(M_{n}\\). In an n-out-of-n empirical bootstrap, the probability of redrawing the original maximum at least once is \\(1-(1-1/n)^{n}\\to 1-e^{-1}\\approx .632\\). Therefore \\(n(M*_{n}-M_{n})\\) has a mass at zero of about .632. The target \\(n(M_{n}-\\theta )\\) has a continuous nonpositive limiting distribution with no mass at zero. The bootstrap fails to reproduce the required distribution. This is the sample-maximum example in Princeton'\\(s\\) bootstrap lecture, p. 12. Boundary problems, some nonsmooth estimators, and dependent sampling can require different resampling procedures. More bootstrap repetitions reduce simulation error, not the underlying approximation error."}, {"file": "13-bootstrap-papers.html", "id": "list", "title": "Bootstrap & Paper Connections · 3. Direct textbook paper connection: List (2003)", "text": "John A. List (2003), “Does Market Experience Eliminate Market Anomalies?” Quarterly Journal of Economics 118(1), 41–71. SW Empirical Exercise E3.2 explicitly cites this paper and describes a sports-collectible trading experiment. Publication details were checked against the publisher record . Connection. Binary trading decisions become Bernoulli outcomes; a trading fraction becomes a sample mean; testing a benchmark becomes proportion inference. The exercise then compares experienced and inexperienced traders. This note uses the experiment as described in SW, and does not claim to reproduce the paper'\\(s\\) full analysis. Derivation. Suppose a share q prefers item A, everyone has stable preferences without an endowment effect, and assignment of A or B is equally likely and independent of preferences. An A-preferring subject trades if assigned B; a B-preferring subject trades if assigned A. The trading probability is \\(q/2+(1-q)/2=.5\\). 中文直觉：50% 的基准来自随机禀赋和稳定偏好的组合，不是因为“人有一半概率喜欢 A”。即便所有人都更喜欢 A，只要一半随机拿到 B，就仍有一半会换。比较 dealer 与 nondealer 则需要留意经验本身是否随机形成。"}, {"file": "13-bootstrap-papers.html", "id": "efron", "title": "Bootstrap & Paper Connections · 4. Method connection: Efron (1979)", "text": "Bradley Efron (1979), “Bootstrap Methods: Another Look at the Jackknife.” Annals of Statistics 7(1), 1–26. DOI / primary publication . Connection. The empirical-bootstrap idea substitutes an estimated population distribution into the sampling experiment. The construction above is supported by Princeton'\\(s\\) lecture §1.2, pp. 3–5, which attributes it to Efron. The original article was not fully read for these notes. Its role here is a methodological origin and a reading lead, not evidence that all bootstrap procedures work."}, {"file": "13-bootstrap-papers.html", "id": "cjn", "title": "Bootstrap & Paper Connections · 5. Research frontier: Cattaneo–Jansson–Nagasawa (2020)", "text": "“Bootstrap-Based Inference for Cube Root Asymptotics.” Econometrica 88(5), 2203–2219. Primary publication DOI . The local paper'\\(s\\) abstract and introduction, pp. 2203–2204, were read. Question. How can inference work for estimators with cube-root rates and non-Gaussian Chernoff-type limits, where the usual empirical bootstrap is inconsistent? The paper proposes modifying the shape of the criterion function used for bootstrap estimation to recover a valid approximation under its conditions. Connection to the core. Consistency alone does not determine a convergence rate; a rate does not determine a normal limit; normal critical values cannot be used without the appropriate limiting distribution. Princeton'\\(s\\) lecture p. 7 explicitly points to this paper as a bootstrap modification. Full theorem assumptions and proofs are beyond this chapter-level note. 中文直觉：本科这条链条常用“\\(\\sqrt{n}\\) 放大后近似正态”。这篇论文说明有些估计量需要不同放大倍数，放大后也不是钟形。它不是推翻 CLT，而是提醒你先检查研究对象是否满足那条定理的结构。"}, {"file": "13-bootstrap-papers.html", "id": "reading-path", "title": "Bootstrap & Paper Connections · 6. Optional reading path", "text": "Start with the empirical-CDF construction and the mean-variance calculation above. Then read Princeton pp. 3–5 for roots and intervals, p. 12 for failure examples, and only then the local Cattaneo–Jansson–Nagasawa introduction. Graham'\\(s\\) Econ 240a syllabus p. 5 places conditional expectation and Bayesian bootstrap in a broader graduate sequence; it is a roadmap rather than proof of any result here. Connection exercise Question. If a bootstrap standard error is stable when B increases from 1,000 to 10,000, does that establish correct confidence-interval coverage? Solution. No. It indicates reduced Monte Carlo error in the chosen bootstrap procedure. The conditional bootstrap law may still approximate the wrong sampling distribution. Sources: SW E3.2, pp. 151–152; Princeton bootstrap lecture pp. 3–5, 7, 12–13; local Cattaneo–Jansson–Nagasawa (2020), pp. 2203–2204; Graham syllabus p. 5. External links identify primary publications."}, {"file": "14-exercises.html", "id": "notation", "title": "Integrated Exercises & Solutions · A. Summation and moment algebra", "text": "A1. Show \\(\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)=0\\) and \\(\\sum_i(x_i-\\bar x)^2=\\sum_i x_i^2-n\\bar x^2\\). Which facts are deterministic? Solution A1 \\(\\sum_i x_i-\\sum_i\\bar x=n\\bar x-n\\bar x=0\\). Expanding the square gives \\(\\sum_i x_i^2-2\\bar x\\sum_i x_i+n\\bar x^2=\\sum_i x_i^2-n\\bar x^2\\). Both hold for any numerical sequence and require no probability model. The elementary rules are \\(\\sum_i a=na\\), \\(\\sum_i ax_i=a\\sum_i x_i\\), and \\(\\sum_i(x_i+y_i)=\\sum_i x_i+\\sum_i y_i\\). A2. For \\(Y\\sim\\)Bernoulli(.3), derive its first four raw moments, variance, skewness, and kurtosis. Solution A2 \\(Y^{r}=Y\\) for every positive integer r, so all four raw moments equal \\(p=.3\\). Variance\\(=p(1-p)=.21\\). The third central moment is \\(p(1-p)(1-2p)\\); skewness\\(=(1-2p)/\\sqrt{p(1-p)}\\approx .87287\\). The fourth central moment is \\(p(1-p)[(1-p)^{3}+p^{3}]\\); kurtosis\\(=[(1-p)^{3}+p^{3}]/[p(1-p)]=1/[p(1-p)]-3\\approx 1.76190\\). Bernoulli need not be leptokurtic. A3. With probability .9 draw S from \\(\\mathcal{N}(0,4)\\), and otherwise from \\(\\mathcal{N}(0,16)\\). Find \\(\\mathbb{E}[S], \\operatorname{Var}(S)\\), skewness, and kurtosis. Why is this relevant to the stock-return box? Solution A3 Condition on the component\\(. \\mathbb{E}[S]=0\\) and \\(\\mathbb{E}[S^{2}]=.9\\cdot 4+.1\\cdot 16=5.2\\). Odd third moments are zero by symmetry\\(. \\mathbb{E}[S^{4}]=.9\\cdot 3\\cdot 4^{2}+.1\\cdot 3\\cdot 16^{2}=120\\). Kurtosis\\(=120/5.2^{2}\\approx 4.43787>3\\). Mixing normal observations with different variances can generate a heavy-tailed marginal distribution. A normal conditional model and a normal marginal model are different claims."}, {"file": "14-exercises.html", "id": "conditional", "title": "Integrated Exercises & Solutions · B. Conditional information and dependence", "text": "B1. Use the commute table in Module 3 to compute \\(\\mathbb{E}[X], \\mathbb{E}[Y], \\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)\\), and Corr(X,Y). Then verify the law of total variance for \\(Y\\). Solution B1 \\(\\mathbb{E}[X]=.7, \\mathbb{E}[Y]=.78, \\mathbb{E}[\\)XY\\(]=.63. \\operatorname{Cov}=.63-.7\\cdot .78=.084. \\operatorname{Var}(X)=.21\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=.1716\\), so \\(\\operatorname{Corr}=.084/\\sqrt{.21\\cdot .1716}\\approx .44250. \\mathbb{E}[\\operatorname{Var}(Y\\mid X)]=.3\\cdot .25+.7\\cdot .09=.138\\); \\(\\operatorname{Var}(\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X])=.0336\\). Their sum is .1716. B2. Let \\(m(X)=\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X], u=Y-m(X)\\), and \\(\\mathbb{E}[Y^{2}]<\\infty\\). Prove \\(\\mathbb{E}[u]=0\\) and \\(\\mathbb{E}[\\)uX]=0 when the product is integrable. Explain why \\(\\mathbb{E}[u]=0\\) alone is insufficient for the second conclusion. Solution B2 \\(\\mathbb{E}[u\\mid X]=0\\). Iterated expectations gives \\(\\mathbb{E}[u]=0\\), and \\(\\mathbb{E}[\\)uX\\(]=\\mathbb{E}[\\)XE\\([u|X]]=0. A\\) counterexample to using only \\(\\mathbb{E}[u]=0\\) is \\(u=X\\) with \\(\\mathbb{E}[X]=0\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Var}(X)>0\\); then \\(\\mathbb{E}[\\)uX\\(]=\\mathbb{E}[X^{2}]>0\\). B3. Independent \\(\\varepsilon\\) and \\(X\\) satisfy \\(\\mathbb{E}[\\varepsilon ]=0, \\operatorname{Var}(\\varepsilon )=1\\); \\(X\\) takes 1 and 2 equally often. With \\(Y=X\\varepsilon\\), determine whether mean independence implies independence here. Solution B3 \\(\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X]=0=\\mathbb{E}[Y]\\), while \\(\\operatorname{Var}(Y|X)=X^{2}\\) takes 1 and 4. Thus the distribution changes with \\(X\\), so independence fails. Mean independence places no constant-variance restriction."}, {"file": "14-exercises.html", "id": "sampling", "title": "Integrated Exercises & Solutions · C. Sampling, consistency, and inference", "text": "C1. For \\(i.i.d. Y_{i}\\sim \\mathcal{N}(10,4)\\), compute \\(\\Pr(9.6\\le \\bar{Y} \\le 10.4)\\) at \\(n=20\\) and \\(n=100\\). Explain whether a CLT is needed. Solution C1 \\(\\operatorname{SD}(\\bar{Y} )=2/\\sqrt{n}\\), so the probability is \\(2\\Phi (.2\\sqrt{n})-1\\). It is approximately .6289 at \\(n=20\\) and .9545 at \\(n=100\\). The result is exact because the observations are normal; no asymptotic approximation is needed. C2. Suppose observations have common variance 9 and pairwise correlation .1. Find \\(\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar{Y} )\\) at \\(n=100\\) and its limit under this common-correlation model. Solution C2 \\(\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar{Y} )=9/100+(99/100)(.1)(9)=.981\\); the limit is .9. The i.i.d. formula would incorrectly give .09. More observations do not eliminate the common component. C3. For \\(n=100, \\bar{Y} =52\\), and \\(s=10\\), test \\(H_{0}\\):\\(\\mu =50\\) against \\((i) \\mu \\ne 50\\) and (ii\\() \\mu >50\\). Construct a 95% interval using the large-sample method. Solution C3 \\(\\operatorname{SE}=1\\) and \\(t=2\\). Two-sided \\(p\\approx .04550\\); upper-sided \\(p\\approx .02275\\). Both reject at 5%. The interval is [50.04,53.96]. The one-sided direction must have been selected in advance; it cannot be chosen to improve significance after seeing \\(t\\). C4. Thirty independent 90% confidence intervals are constructed for the same fixed parameter. What is the expected number covering it? What is the probability all cover, if each has exactly 90% coverage? Solution C4 The expected count is 30(.9)=27 by linearity; independence is not needed for that expectation. Independence gives \\(\\Pr(\\)all cover\\()=.9^{30}\\approx .04239\\). Marginal confidence levels do not automatically provide simultaneous coverage. C5. Construct an unbiased estimator of \\(\\mu ^{2}\\) from \\(\\bar{Y}\\) and \\(s^{2}\\) under i.i.d. finite-variance sampling. Is \\(\\bar{Y} ^{2}\\) consistent? Solution C5 \\(\\mathbb{E}[\\bar{Y} ^{2}]=\\mu ^{2}+\\sigma ^{2}/n\\) and \\(\\mathbb{E}[s^{2}]=\\sigma ^{2}\\), so \\(\\bar{Y} ^{2}-s^{2}/n\\) is unbiased for \\(\\mu ^{2}\\). Also \\(\\bar{Y} ^{2}\\xrightarrow{p} \\mu ^{2}\\) by continuous mapping. The unbiased estimator can be negative in a realized sample even though \\(\\mu ^{2}\\ge 0\\). C6. Suppose \\(Y_{i}\\) are i.i.d. normal with known \\(\\sigma =10\\) and \\(n=100. A\\) one-sided 5% test compares \\(H_{0}\\):\\(\\mu =50\\) with \\(\\mu >50\\). Find its cutoff for \\(\\bar{Y}\\) and its power at \\(\\mu =52\\). What happens to power if the significance level is reduced to 1%? Solution C6 \\(\\operatorname{SD}(\\bar{Y} )=1\\), so reject above 50+1.64485=51.64485. At \\(\\mu =52\\), power\\(=1-\\Phi (51.64485-52)=\\Phi (.35515)\\approx .6388\\). At 1%, the cutoff is 52.32635 and power\\(=1-\\Phi (.32635)\\approx .3721\\). Reducing Type I error raises the hurdle and lowers power at this fixed alternative."}, {"file": "14-exercises.html", "id": "design", "title": "Integrated Exercises & Solutions · D. Group comparisons and identification", "text": "D1. Two independent groups have (\\(n\\),mean\\(,\\operatorname{SD})=(100,12,4\\)) and (80,10,5). Estimate their difference and construct a large-sample 95% interval. State which assumption allows variances to add. Solution D1 Difference=2; \\(\\operatorname{SE}=\\sqrt{16/100+25/80}=\\sqrt{.4725}\\approx .68739\\). The interval is [ .6527,3.3473 ]. Independence between group samples sets covariance between their means to zero. Equal variances are unnecessary. D2. For 64 independent pairs, the mean before–after change is 3 and the SD of the changes is 8. Compute a 95% interval. Does it identify a treatment effect? Solution D2 \\(\\operatorname{SE}=8/8=1\\); interval=[1.04,4.96]. This measures uncertainty about the mean change. Without a design that rules out other causes, practice and time effects can explain the change. D3. Show why equal two-group sample sizes equate pooled and unpooled SEs. Does this prove an exact Student \\(t\\) null distribution? Solution D3 At common size \\(m, s_{p}^{2}=(s_{A}^{2}+s_{B}^{2})/2\\). Multiply by \\(2/m\\) to get \\(SE_{p}^{2}=(s_{A}^{2}+s_{B}^{2})/m\\), equal to the unpooled variance estimate. Exact \\(t\\) still generally requires normality, independence, and common population variance."}, {"file": "14-exercises.html", "id": "connections", "title": "Integrated Exercises & Solutions · E. Theorem and paper connections", "text": "E1. In \\(Y=a+\\)bX\\(+U\\), let \\(\\operatorname{Var}(X)=4, \\operatorname{Cov}(X,U)=2\\), and \\(b=3\\). What does \\(\\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)/\\operatorname{Var}(X)\\) equal? What restrictions on \\(Z\\) would recover b through another covariance ratio? Solution E1 The observed ratio is 3+2/4=3.5. If \\(\\operatorname{Cov}(Z,U)=0\\) and \\(\\operatorname{Cov}(Z,X)\\ne 0\\), then \\(\\operatorname{Cov}(Z,Y)/\\operatorname{Cov}(Z,X)=3. A\\) numerical correlation with \\(X\\) alone does not establish the exclusion restriction. E2. In the sports-collectible design, let the probability of receiving A be r rather than .5, independently of preferences. A fraction q prefers A. Derive the no-endowment-effect trading rate. Solution E2 The trading rate is \\(q(1-r)+(1-q)r\\). At \\(r=.5\\) it equals .5 for every q. Otherwise it depends on preferences, so testing against .5 without checking assignment probabilities can be wrong. E3. Why does resampling the sample maximum produce a mass at zero in the centered bootstrap statistic? What kind of error remains as \\(B\\to \\infty\\)? Solution E3 The original maximum is included with probability \\(1-(1-1/n)^{n}\\); whenever included, the bootstrap maximum equals it. Increasing B estimates this bootstrap mass more accurately but does not remove the mismatch with the target continuous distribution. E4. Use LLN, continuous mapping, and Slutsky to explain the sequence “estimate variance \\(\\to\\) studentize \\(\\to\\) use normal quantiles.” Identify where a zero population variance would cause trouble. Solution E4 LLNs give \\(s^{2}\\xrightarrow{p} \\sigma ^{2}\\). For \\(\\sigma ^{2}>0\\), continuous mapping yields \\(\\sigma /s\\xrightarrow{p} 1\\). The CLT gives \\(\\sqrt{n}(\\bar{Y} -\\mu )/\\sigma \\xrightarrow{d} \\mathcal{N}(0,1)\\); Slutsky gives the same limit after replacing \\(\\sigma\\) by \\(s\\). At \\(\\sigma ^{2}=0\\), standardization divides by zero and this argument is unavailable. Exercise design draws on SW Exercises 2.4, 2.13, 2.15, 2.19–2.27; 3.5–3.6, 3.15–3.21; Schellenberg Section 2; and Princeton bootstrap p. 12. Questions are adapted or newly constructed. All solutions are supplied locally."}, {"file": "15-empirical-workflows.html", "id": "joint-table", "title": "Empirical Workflows · 1. Joint-distribution workflow: age and earnings", "text": "SW E2.1 starts from a joint probability table for Age and AHE. First validate that cell probabilities are nonnegative and sum to one. Then: Sum each age row across earnings to obtain \\(p_{A}(a)\\). Divide the row'\\(s\\) joint cells by \\(p_{A}(a)\\) to obtain the conditional earnings PMF. Compute \\(m(a)=\\sum _{y}\\)yP\\((AHE=y|Age=a)\\). Plot a against m(a), labeling units and population restrictions. Compute \\(\\mathbb{E}[AHE]=\\sum _{a}m(a)p_{A}(a)\\), and check against the marginal-earnings calculation. Compute \\(\\mathbb{E}[AHE^{2}]\\), variance\\(, \\mathbb{E}[Age\\cdot AHE]\\), covariance, and correlation. Compare the correlation with the conditional-mean plot; curvature can make them tell different stories. Self-contained substitute example. Let Age be 25 or 30 and earnings be 10 or 20. Joint probabilities are (.3,.2) for age 25 and (.1,.4) for age 30. Each age has mass .5; conditional means are 14 and \\(18. \\mathbb{E}[AHE]=16, \\operatorname{Var}(AHE)=24, \\mathbb{E}[Age]=27.5, \\operatorname{Var}(Age)=6.25, \\mathbb{E}[Age\\cdot AHE]=445\\), and covariance=5. Correlation\\(=5/\\sqrt{150}\\approx .40825\\). These are pedagogical numbers, not CPS data. 中文直觉：这条练习把概率表、条件期望、总体平均数和相关系数串在一起。两个算法算出来的总体平均数应该相同，这是一个很便宜但有用的核查。"}, {"file": "15-empirical-workflows.html", "id": "cps", "title": "Empirical Workflows · 2. CPS earnings workflow", "text": "SW Appendix 3.1 describes the Current Population Survey underlying the book'\\(s\\) historical examples. Its actual sampling design is more complex than a literal i.i.d. draw. For real microdata analysis, definitions, weights, and design-based uncertainty must be checked. The textbook exercises use a simplified instructional approach. In the supplied appendix, the earnings examples use the March survey'\\(s\\) information on earnings in the preceding year. Full-time workers are defined there as working more than 35 hours per week for at least 48 weeks in that preceding year. Average hourly earnings combine annual pretax wages, salaries, tips, and bonuses and divide by annual hours. The appendix describes geographic sampling followed by housing-unit sampling; these are the book'\\(s\\) historical data definitions, not a statement of current survey specifications. Read the data dictionary; identify year, education, earnings, hours, and the analysis sample. Document exclusions, missing values, full-time criteria, and whether weights are required. Compute mean, SD, and \\(n\\) by year in nominal units. Convert earnings to a common price level before comparing purchasing power. Compute intervals for each mean and for differences using the appropriate design. Compare education groups within each year, then compare the education gap across years. Report estimands, units, uncertainty, and limits on causal interpretation. For independent year-by-group samples, a change in an education gap is \\((\\bar{Y} _{college,2}-\\bar{Y} _{HS,2})-(\\bar{Y} _{college,1}-\\bar{Y} _{HS,1})\\), with the four mean variances added. If observations overlap or survey clusters link the estimates, include the corresponding covariances instead. A reporting sentence should identify the difference, the confidence interval, the price year, and the population. “The gap changed significantly” is incomplete without those definitions."}, {"file": "15-empirical-workflows.html", "id": "sportscards", "title": "Empirical Workflows · 3. Sports-card experiment workflow", "text": "SW E3.2 uses the List (2003) experiment described in Module 13. Given the data, define \\(Trade=1\\) for trading and zero otherwise. Verify coding, assignment probabilities, and missing outcomes. Estimate the trading proportion and compare it with the justified no-endowment-effect benchmark. Next estimate separate trading proportions for dealers and nondealers, and directly test their difference. Do not infer a significant group difference just because one group'\\(s\\) benchmark test rejects and the other'\\(s\\) does not. Distinguish randomized initial endowment from nonrandom market experience. Data status. The required external dataset is a separate input. The learning text supplies the estimator, standard-error logic, benchmark derivation, and interpretation workflow; it does not report uncomputed trading fractions."}, {"file": "15-empirical-workflows.html", "id": "monte-carlo", "title": "Empirical Workflows · 4. A Monte Carlo workflow that needs no external dataset", "text": "SW Exercise 2.16 approximates an event probability by simulation. If \\(Y^{(b)}\\) are independent draws from a specified distribution, then: \\[\\hat p_B=\\frac1B\\sum_{b=1}^B\\mathbf1\\{Y^{(b)}<c\\}\\xrightarrow{p}P(Y<c).\\] The indicator is Bernoulli, so Monte Carlo \\(\\operatorname{SE}\\approx \\sqrt{\\hat{p} _{B}(1-\\hat{p} _{B})/B}\\). Here B controls simulation precision, while \\(n\\) controls the sampling precision of an estimator within each simulated dataset. These are separate sample sizes. To study interval coverage, fix a known \\(\\mu\\), draw \\(B\\) datasets of size \\(n\\), construct one interval per dataset, and average \\(\\mathbf 1\\{\\mu\\text{ is covered}\\}\\). Compare coverage, average interval width, and rejection rates across \\(n\\) and population shapes. The sampling laboratory in Module 6 implements the repeated-sampling idea locally. Sources: SW E2.1, p. 109; E3.1, pp. 149–150; E3.2, pp. 151–152; Appendix 3.1, p. 152; Exercise 2.16; Schellenberg Section 2, pp. 7–9. Synthetic table and reporting structure are original teaching additions."}, {"file": "16-reference.html", "id": "notation", "title": "Notation & Formula Reference · 1. Notation", "text": "Symbol Meaning Y, X Population random variables \\(Y_{i}, X_{i}\\) Random observation i; lowercase symbols may denote realizations \\(\\mu , \\sigma ^{2}, \\sigma , \\sigma _{XY}, \\rho _{XY}\\) Population mean, variance, SD, covariance, correlation \\(\\bar{Y} , s^{2}, s, s_{XY}, r_{XY}\\) Sample analogues; random before the sample is observed \\(\\hat{\\theta } , \\hat{\\delta } , \\hat{p}\\) Estimator of a general parameter, a difference, a proportion \\(\\operatorname{SE}(\\hat{\\theta } )\\) Estimated sampling standard deviation of \\(\\hat{\\theta }\\) \\(\\xrightarrow{p} , \\xrightarrow{d}\\) Convergence in probability; convergence in distribution \\(\\Phi , z_{q}\\) Standard normal CDF; its q-quantile \\(\\mathbb{E}*, \\operatorname{Var}*, \\hat{\\theta } *\\) Conditional bootstrap expectation, variance, and estimate"}, {"file": "16-reference.html", "id": "formula", "title": "Notation & Formula Reference · 2. Formula sheet", "text": "Result Formula Condition / module Expectation of a function \\(\\mathbb{E}[g(Y)]=\\sum g(y)p(y)\\) Integrability; 02 Variance identity \\(\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=\\mathbb{E}[Y^{2}]-\\mathbb{E}[Y]^{2}\\) Finite second moment; 02 Iterated expectations \\(\\mathbb{E}[Y]=\\mathbb{E}[\\mathbb{E}[Y\\mid X]]\\) \\(\\mathbb{E}|Y|<\\infty\\); 03 Total variance \\(\\operatorname{Var}(Y)=\\mathbb{E}[\\operatorname{Var}(Y|X)]+\\operatorname{Var}(\\mathbb{E}[Y|X])\\) Finite second moment; 03 Variance of a difference \\(\\operatorname{Var}(X-Y)=\\operatorname{Var}(X)+\\operatorname{Var}(Y)-2Cov(X,Y)\\) Finite variances; 04 Mean sampling variance \\(\\operatorname{Var}(\\bar{Y} )=\\sigma ^{2}/n\\) i.i.d., finite variance; 06 LLN \\(\\bar{Y} \\xrightarrow{p} \\mu\\) i.i.d., finite variance suffices; 06 CLT \\(\\sqrt{n}(\\bar{Y} -\\mu )/\\sigma \\xrightarrow{d} \\mathcal{N}(0,1)\\) \\(i.i.d., 0<\\sigma ^{2}<\\infty\\); 06 MSE \\(\\operatorname{Var}(\\hat{\\theta } )+\\operatorname{Bias}(\\hat{\\theta } )^{2}\\) Finite second error moment; 07 Sample variance \\(s^{2}=\\sum (Y_{i}-\\bar{Y} )^{2}/(n-1)\\) \\(n\\ge 2\\); unbiased under i.i.d.; 08 Mean SE \\(s/\\sqrt{n}\\) i.i.d. model; 08 Two-sided p-value \\(2[1-\\Phi (|t|)]\\) Normal reference justified; 09 Mean confidence interval \\(\\bar{Y} \\pm z_{1-\\alpha /2}\\operatorname{SE}\\) Large-sample route; 09 Independent-group SE \\(\\sqrt{s_{A}^{2}/n_{A}+s_{B}^{2}/n_{B}}\\) Independent groups; 10 Paired SE \\(s_{D}/\\sqrt{n}\\) Independent pairs; 10 Exact mean interval \\(\\bar{Y} \\pm t_{n-1,1-\\alpha /2}s/\\sqrt{n}\\) i.i.d. normal population; 11 Sample correlation \\(s_{XY}/(s_{X}s_{Y})\\) Positive sample SDs; 12 OLS slope with intercept \\(\\hat{b} =s_{XY}/s_{X}^{2}\\) Nonconstant sample \\(X\\); 12"}, {"file": "16-reference.html", "id": "pitfalls", "title": "Notation & Formula Reference · 3. Common mistakes and repairs", "text": "Incorrect shortcut Correct distinction “A density height is a probability.” Continuous probabilities are areas. “\\(\\mathbb{E}[g(Y)]=g(\\mathbb{E}[Y])\\).” Linearity works for affine g; nonlinear transformations need separate calculations. “Zero correlation means independence.” It removes linear association; joint normality is a special exception. “The CLT makes observations normal.” It approximates a centered and scaled average. “An unbiased estimator must be consistent.” \\(Y_{1}\\) is a counterexample. “SE is \\(s/n\\).” For the i.i.d. sample mean it is \\(s/\\sqrt{n}\\). “A t-statistic always has a t distribution.” Exact \\(t\\) requires distributional assumptions. “\\(p=.04\\) means a 4% chance \\(H_{0}\\) is true.” It is a tail probability computed under \\(H_{0}\\). “95% confidence covers 95% of people.” An interval for a mean describes estimation uncertainty. “Subtract standard errors for a difference.” Combine variances, including covariance when needed, then take a square root. “Significant in one group but not another proves a difference.” Test the difference directly. “A precise observational gap proves causality.” Identification and sampling precision are separate. “More bootstrap draws fix invalid bootstrap inference.” They reduce Monte Carlo error only. 中文复习方法：看到公式先说出“随机的是谁、目标是谁、分母是什么、依赖什么条件”。如果四个问题答不清楚，先回到相应模块的推导，再做计算。"}, {"file": "17-sources-coverage.html", "id": "primary", "title": "Sources & Coverage Map · 1. Primary sources and page conventions", "text": "Stock and Watson, supplied Global Edition chapter extracts: Chapter 2, printed pp. 60–110 (51 PDF pages); Chapter 3, printed pp. 111–154 (44 PDF pages). Thus Chapter 2 printed page = PDF page + 59; Chapter 3 printed page = PDF page + 110. Chapter 2 PDF · Chapter 3 PDF · Econ 141 syllabus The syllabus's first topic is probability/statistics review and emphasizes mathematical derivations. The chapter files, rather than a different edition's page numbers, govern this guide."}, {"file": "17-sources-coverage.html", "id": "support", "title": "Sources & Coverage Map · 2. Supporting materials actually consulted", "text": "Schellenberg Section 1 , pp. 1–2: probability functions. Section 2 , pp. 4–8: covariance ratios, LIE, LLN/CLT, sample versus population, and Monte Carlo. These are historical supplementary sections, not the current syllabus. Aden Chen econometrics notes , pp. 7, 12–14, 16, 23: conditional prediction, continuous mapping, Slutsky, moments, and OLS asymptotics. Princeton ECO 539B bootstrap lecture , pp. 3–5, 7, 12–13: empirical resampling, studentized intervals, and failure examples. Graham Econ 240a syllabus , p. 5: graduate-course roadmap. It is not used as a proof source. Cattaneo, Jansson, and Nagasawa (2020), Bootstrap-Based Inference for Cube Root Asymptotics , local PDF pp. 1–2 (printed 2203–2204): abstract/introduction read; full theorem proofs not reviewed."}, {"file": "17-sources-coverage.html", "id": "scope", "title": "Sources & Coverage Map · 3. What was consolidated", "text": "Repeated descriptions of the same definition, duplicate numerical drills, and repeated normal-curve explanations were consolidated. All 13 main sections (2.1–2.6 and 3.1–3.7), all four appendices, and all three empirical-exercise pathways have a destination. Historical application tables are discussed through their inferential purpose rather than reproduced in full. Every numbered textbook exercise is not duplicated; its distinct conceptual chains are represented by the main text and curated exercises."}, {"file": "17-sources-coverage.html", "id": "coverage", "title": "Sources & Coverage Map · 4. Coverage map", "text": "Original location Knowledge chain Destination Status SW §2.1 · pp. 61–65 Outcomes → events → random variables → PMF/CDF/PDF → event probabilities 01 covered SW §2.2 · pp. 65–71 Expectation → Bernoulli moments → variance/SD → affine changes → skewness/kurtosis/raw moments 02 , 14 covered SW §2.3 · pp. 72–81 Joint → marginal → conditional → conditional moments → LIE → zero conditional mean 03 covered SW §2.3 · pp. 77–81 Independence → mean independence → covariance/correlation → weighted-sum algebra 04 covered SW Table 2.4 · pp. 79–80 Conditional earnings distributions → means/medians/spread → subgroup definitions 02 , 10 covered SW §2.4 · pp. 82–88 Normal standardization → multivariate normal properties → chi-squared → t → F and limits 05 covered SW stock-return box · pp. 85–86 Extreme observations → normal-model inadequacy → tails/variable volatility 02 , 14 covered SW §2.5 · pp. 89–93 Sampling design → i.i.d. → sampling distribution → exact mean moments → diversification 04 , 06 covered SW §2.6 · pp. 93–99 Exact versus asymptotic → LLN/consistency → standardized CLT → approximation quality 06 covered SW Exercises 2.19–2.27 · pp. 106–109 Multiple conditioning → moment expansions → portfolio → nonlinear dependence → summation → correlated averages → best prediction 02 , 03 , 04 , 05 , 14 covered; recurring calculations consolidated SW Appendix 2.1 · pp. 109–110 Variance/covariance identities → expansion proofs → correlation inequality 02 , 04 covered SW E2.1 · p. 109 Joint earnings table → conditional means → LIE → covariance/correlation 15 workflow and synthetic worked example; original dataset unavailable SW §3.1 · pp. 112–117 Estimator versus estimate → bias/consistency/efficiency → BLUE → least squares → selection bias 07 covered SW §3.2 · pp. 117–126 Null/alternative → known/unknown variance → sample variance/SE → t statistic → p-value → size/power → sidedness 08 , 09 covered SW §3.3 · pp. 126–128 Test inversion → confidence set/interval → coverage → confidence levels 09 covered SW §3.4 · pp. 128–130 Independent means → variance of difference → SE → tests/intervals 10 covered SW §3.5 and applications · pp. 130–133,136 Randomized experiment → conditional-mean causal effect → treatment estimation → natural experiments; earnings gap/time comparison 10 covered SW §3.6 · pp. 133–137 Normal-sample t → pooled variance → equality-of-variance restriction → large-sample practice 11 covered with source correction SW §3.7 · pp. 137–141 Scatterplots → sample covariance/correlation → consistency → unit invariance → nonlinear relationships 12 covered SW Exercises 3.2–3.21 · pp. 143–149 Proportions → precision planning → power → paired comparisons → gap changes → variance unbiasedness → transformations → covariance consistency → pooled equality 08 , 09 , 10 , 11 , 12 , 14 covered; repeated numerical drills consolidated SW E3.1 · pp. 149–150 CPS means → real earnings → education/year comparisons → gap changes 15 complete analysis recipe; raw dataset unavailable SW E3.2 · pp. 151–152 Endowment experiment → 50% benchmark → Bernoulli test → experience-group comparison → List (2003) 13 , 14 , 15 benchmark derived; raw dataset unavailable SW Appendix 3.1 · p. 152 CPS source/design → full-time definition → population and weighting caveats 15 covered SW Appendix 3.2 · p. 153 Least-squares mean: calculus and algebraic proofs 07 covered SW Appendix 3.3 · p. 154 Second sample moment minus squared mean → LLN → consistent variance 08 covered"}, {"file": "17-sources-coverage.html", "id": "corrections", "title": "Sources & Coverage Map · 5. Corrections and clarifications preserved in the notes", "text": "The extended-reading README's “no formal citations” statement is incorrect when applied to the complete Chapter 3: E3.2 explicitly cites List (2003). Chapter 2's stock-return box also mentions Taleb's book. The original README was left unchanged; this guide records the correction. Equation (3.23), printed p. 135, uses the first group's mean inside the second group's squared deviations in the supplied PDF. The pooled variance must use each group's own mean. This was checked visually against the PDF. Equal group sizes equate pooled and unpooled SE formulas but do not, by themselves, establish an exact Student t distribution. The exact n−1 sample-variance convention for a Bernoulli sample differs slightly from the usual n-divisor plug-in proportion SE. Both are stated explicitly. SD(a+bY)=|b|SD(Y); the absolute value is needed for negative b. Symmetry implies zero skewness when the moment exists, but the converse is not universal. Finite-variance LLN and finite-fourth-moment sample-variance arguments are sufficient conditions used in the chapter. They are not stated as the weakest possible assumptions. Simple random sampling from a finite population without replacement is not literally i.i.d.; the guide distinguishes the textbook's working model from this design issue. Convergence in probability can be to a random variable in general. The LLN's constant limit is the case used here, not a universal definition."}, {"file": "17-sources-coverage.html", "id": "limits", "title": "Sources & Coverage Map · 6. Evidence and limits", "text": "The original exercise microdatasets were not found in the inspected local files. Empirical workflows and explicitly synthetic examples are supplied; no new CPS or sports-card estimates are claimed. Efron is an attributed reading lead based on the Princeton notes, not a full-paper review. List's publication metadata was checked against its publisher; the experiment description comes from SW. The Cattaneo–Jansson–Nagasawa introduction was read locally. Advanced extensions are marked and are not presented as original chapter requirements. Source hashes, build checks, numerical checks, and editorial decisions are stored separately in the course Agent Workspace. The reader works offline: typography, navigation, search, and simulation use local assets."}];